If you need a cdrom as I did.  Make sure it reads 512 byte blocks.  I bought a
scsi cdrom at frys and it will not work.  The people at several stores did not
know what I was talking about when I asked about 512 byte blocks.  I had an old
Toshiba 3401 at work and with a small modification it will work at 512 byte
blocks (so says their web site).

I also bought some more ram.  It must be parity ram.  NOT EDO or fast page ram.
It is fairly expensive.  I did buy a cheep PCI video card.  I also bought a
update utility from digital.  1-800-digital.  It is also downloadable.  Most
likely unuseable to you though.  You must have a bootable unix floppy to use it
and I am not sure how you would change the bios if needed.  (UNIX or NT) They
use a different bios depending on which operating system you will want to use.
I have not gotten mine running yet.  Have had it a few months so far.

