In article <368a6add.3779004@newse.twcny.rr.com>, 
rmcconne.NOSPAM@lightlink.com (Robert McConnell) writes:

> I have been given an LN03+ which I have been able to connect to my
> Linux system and print plain text files. But it has a blank streak in
> columns 77-79, 

I suspect that it is caused by a build-up of paper "fluff"
in the area of the magnetic toner roller.   Pull out the
toner/OPC drawer.   Remove the OPC belt and carefully set
it in a dark place.   Then look at the roller that would
press against the OPC belt.   It is magnetic, and should
have an even coating of toner particles all across its
width.

What often happens is that paper (and other) "fluff" gets
into the toner bin and collects between the roller and
the bin edge just above the roller.   If this is your
problem, you will see an uneven area along the length
of the roller.

There are two ways to fix it.   The quickest (and most
hazardous to your printer) is to cut a small piece of
shim stock (I use .005" brass) into a hook, and try to
slide it just above the roller and pull the fluff out.

The "correct" way is to dump out all excess toner and
thoroughly clean the entire assembly.   If you use a
vacuum cleaner, do it outdoors and immediately throw
the bag away as soon as you are done.

