In comp.os.linux.alpha Steve Lionel <Steve.Lionel@digital.com> wrote:
> I asked a question, I didn't make a statement.  I'm trying to help our
> management gather information that will help them make a decision.  Most of
> the people who have written to us name the software licensing fees for
> DIGITAL UNIX as the major reason they are using Linux.  *I* know that that's
> not the only reason one uses Linux, but we need to hear it from users.

> As far as any "petition" goes - we haven't seen any formal collection of
> requests.

Nor are you likely to, I think. IMHO, the biggest market for this particular
setup would be the educational-scientific one. I'm particularly thinking
of a large number of physics departments where physicists still prefer to 
write in fortran, and need the number crunching abilities of the alpha.

I know a few people in this crowd -- they don't seem like the types
to put together a petition for this. The fact is (no offense intended),
that they seldom seem to think of anything besides physics, so if you
want their attention you will have to go to them. 


> Compaq would very much like to encourage use of Alpha systems, and Compaq's
> upper management thinks Linux is a good way to broaden the Alpha market.  No
> argument from me on that!  We have come up with estimates on what it would
> cost us to offer a F90 product on Alpha Linux, and presented them to
> management.  What would influence them in favor of the proposal is to know
> that there are a large number of customers who would pay a reasonable price
> ($500 is the figure most users I've corresponded with have centered on) for
> a compiler, and that the support costs wouldn't eat us alive.  (We are
> concerned about the idea that everyone's Linux system is different from
> everyone else's...)

$500 is very reasonable to places used to paying several thousand. If you want
this to happen (and it seems like you do), you have to go to the people who
will buy it. I think a number of them read this group, even though they haven't
spoken up. But I think a large portion of them are totally ignorant of this
group, of Linux's potential, and even of the alpha's potential. They are
happy to use whatever computing resources their dept. gives them, and will
just complain to their sysadmin if there isn't enough to go around. 

I'd love to see this happen. I'd love to see the Digital C compilers
available for alpha as well, since gcc and egcs are (apparently) so lacking.
I'm not it much of a position to help though. What I would suggest
that you do is make some contacts, or heck, go to some of the *many* large
physics conferences, and show your goods (alpha, DU + Fortran), and then
when they gripe about the price say "well.. there is always linux...".

I'm sure Compaq already knows the benifits of getting a foothold in the
educational market.. 

Paul





