In article <VA.00000402.01c1250e@pluto.girdglen>, Ray James <Ray.James@surf24.de> writes:
:What is the approximate computing power of an AlphaStation 200 4/166 ?

  Cross-architecture open-ended performance questions are difficult to 
  answer, unless you're in marketing.  :-)  If you are accustomed to an 
  AlphaStation 255 300, it's slow.  If you're used to an AlphaStation
  200 4/100, it's pretty speedy.  You *will* want to configure sufficient
  physical memory -- I'd recommend at least 64 MB, and more is obviously
  better -- to really get this system to haul...

:Just a ballpark figure, compared to Intel Pentium systems.

  You might be better served by comparing a standard benchmark (such
  as the SPECmumble benchmarks), rather than requesting a nebulous
  comparison of one system to a whole range of systems from different
  families of a wholely different architecture -- and, for example,
  are you planning to run Windows NT binaries for Intel, or do you
  have FX!32 or Alpha binaries available to you?  In other words,
  there are many factors to this comparision, far too many to have
  a concise answer that has any bearing on reality...

  Try looking around at http://www.spec.org/, as a starting point...

:Incidentally, what do the 200 and 4 numbers stand for ?

  The mode name architecture has varied (widely) over the years, but 
  the secret decoder ring for these systems were the 200 or 255 series
  enclosures, with an EV4 (the 21064 or its derivatives) microprocessor 
  running at circa 166 Mhz.

:What is the difference between the AS 200 and the AS 255?

  "AS" is ambiguous, it could be an AlphaStation, an AlphaServer, or
  (with the correct digits after it) a series of systems from IBM...
  The AlphaStation 255 fixed several tight-clearance complaints with
  the internals of the AlphaStation 200, and the AlphaStation 255 has
  support for faster versions of the EV4 processor.

  Most AlphaStation 255 systems, as well as the later members of the
  200 series are what are called "half-flash", meaning that there is
  an extra step involved if you are moving between the Windows NT 
  console and the console for the UNIX and OpenVMS operating systems.

:Is there a list anywhere of the various AlphaStation models, when they 
:came out, and their relative power to each other?

  The following is a mix of AlphaServer and AlphaStation URLs, with
  the SPEC URL repeated:

http://www.workstation.digital.com/products/mature.html
http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/solutions/ibi/oldspecs/old_system_specs.html
http://www.digital.com/info/alphaserver/archive/archive_comparison.html
http://www.spec.org/


