On Thu, 31 Dec 1998, Ray James wrote:
> What is the approximate computing power of an AlphaStation 200 4/166 ?

Not a whole lot, nowadays.
 
> Just a ballpark figure, compared to Intel Pentium systems.

Here are SPEC number to show a comparison:

http://www.spec.org/osg/cpu95/results/cpu95.html

CINT95
Company           System                    Result Baseline     
Digital Equipmen  AlphaStation 200 4/166    2.31   2.31
Digital Equipmen  AlphaStation 200 4/166    2.95   2.45
Intel Corporatio  Intel LT430TX (133MHz)    4.01   4.01        
Intel Corporatio  Intel LT430TX (133MHz)    3.95   3.95

This shows the integer performance to be quite a bit less than a Pentium 133.
Probably slightly lower than a P90 even.

CFP95
Company           System                    Result Baseline     
Digital Equipmen  AlphaStation 200 4/166    3.64   3.30
Intel Corporatio  Intel LT430TX (150MHz)    3.53   2.57
Intel Corporatio  Intel LT430TX (166MHz)    3.92   2.86

Looks like floating point performance is about Pentium 150-166 MHz range.     

> Incidentally, what do the 200 and 4 numbers stand for ?
> What is the difference between the AS 200 and the AS 255?

4 is the CPU family ID.  4 means a 21064 Alpha chip.  The 21064 is the 
first-generation of Alpha that was released to consumers. 

200 is a model number.  Looks like the big difference between the 200 and the
255 series is that the 255 model series includes 21064a Alphas.  They are
slightly faster than 21064 chips.  Mostly because they include more cache
memory on chip.

Check out http://www.workstation.digital.com/products/mature.html for the 
details.  These workstations are "mature" products as far as DEC^H^H^HCompaq
is concerned.

> Is there a list anywhere of the various AlphaStation models, when they 
> came out, and their relative power to each other?

Check out the above website.

