SMS@Provis.com wrote:
> 
> > From: "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com>
> > Not directly on the subject, but has any progress been made re: OpenVMS
> > Hobbyist Licenses for Alpha customers? Seems to me that there are plenty
> > of el cheapo first or second generation Alpha systems out there in
> > various home environments; ...
> 
>    I, for one, would be willing to pay up to a few hundred dollars for
> VMS for my (Onsale) AlphaStation 200 4/233.  If I could afford to switch
> from Linux, I'd do it in a minute (plus load time).  I spent as much for
> a VMS 7.2 field test kit as I did for Red Hat Linux (5.1) just to see
> what I was missing (until all the temporary PAK's died).
> 
>    However, the current VAX-only hobbyist scheme omits a few critical
> items, such as DECnet, cluster, DECwindows/Motif, and TCP/IP licenses,
> without which much of the appeal is missing.

TCP/IP would be useful, but how many home users envision setting up a
cluster? (Yeah, I know, there's gotta be SOMEONE out there...)

> 
>    I can understand why any entity with valuable intelectual property
> would be reluctant to give it away, but if the market/mind-share of VMS
> dwindles endlessly, what happens to that value?

It craters, of course. Even a Goals 2000 student knows that.

> 
>    What's different at Sun, where they're (practically) giving away
> their latest-and-greatest Solaris 7 (SunOS 5.7) for non-commercial use?
> (See "http://www.sun.com/developers/solarispromo.html".)  The casual
> observer might suspect that one of these organizations is smarter than
> the other one.  (I know which one I'd pick.  Certainly, one has been
> more successful than the other.)
> 

Amen. And it would behoove CPQ to take a good look at why this is the
case. As I wrote in SKD last August 21:


Linus does it. Scott's doing it. Maybe Jesse ought to consider doing it,
too.

"Give it Away," the Red Hot Chili Peppers once 'crooned. And that's
exactly what's happening with Linux and now, under the right conditions,
Solaris.

It's also what�s happening with NetBSD, and, under the right conditions,
even OpenVMS. For the last couple of years, an OpenVMS Hobbyist License
has been available gratis to those who consort with VAXen in the privacy
of their own homes. There is, of course, plenty of fine print: the
Hobbyist License applies only to VAXen, and such freeware-equipped iron
can not be used for any commercial venture. Nevertheless, the DECUS
DFWLUG has done a superb job proliferating OVMS to advocates who might
otherwise scuttle their VAXen and surrender to the evil empire.

Given the unflagging interest in OpenVMS on the desktop, as evidenced in
Usenet newsgroups--and in SKD's numerous discussions with end-users
throughout the world--now might be a good time for Compaq to strongly
consider extending the Hobbyist License concept to Alpha-based desktops. 
Linux has driven the sale of many an AlphaStation, and OpenVMS is sure
to stimulate further demand. Consider Sun's success with its Free
Solaris Program. During the first week of the initiative, the court of
Le Roi Soleil saw over 10K requests for almost-gratis Solaris. Unlike
the OVMS Hobbyist scheme, Sun's program allows users to receive a
license for the latest version of the Solaris OS for non-commercial use
on Intel or Sparc-based workstations. In both cases, users pay for
media, shipping and handling.

Whether Scott is attempting to bury a few carefully-placed antipersonnel
mines in the Microsoft playing field, or whether he's trying to preempt
Linux, remains uncertain. And irrelevant. At the end of the day, Sun
will garner a significantly larger user base for its flagship OS. Does
it not make sense for Compaq to do the same thing with OpenVMS, an OS
responsible for over $120B in aggregate hardware, software, and service
revenue?

I submit that it does. Compaq justifies the hefty fees it charges for
OpenVMS on the desktop to development, certification, and ongoing
support costs. Considering that the incremental expense of rendering
OpenVMS safe for the Monet platforms of the world is modest, why not
make this option attractive to low-end users who swear by OVMS and swear
at all other OSes? Why not emulate Scott McNealy by giving 'em the OS
and charging for the support? (While doing so, a vendor of course must
ensure that a giveaway OS doesn't find its way onto a commercial server,
but that's what license brokers are for!)

There might not be any immediate incremental revenue to be realized from
the adoption of such a stance, but let's not forget that attaching a
hefty premium to the price of OpenVMS desktops will merely cause more
users to be assimilated by the Borg of Redmond. A strategy of
appeasement will do as much good for Compaq as it did for Neville
Chamberlain. What's more, if OpenVMS ever had a Maginot Line, it was
gutted in the name of a one-sided "Alliance" for Enterprise Computing.

Looking down "The Road Ahead," Bill Gates has one map with one
destination, which happens to be in the Greater Redmond area. For Compaq
cartographers to acquiesce to a roadmap to an empire ruled by a
not-so-benevolent despot flies in the face of maintaining an active
OpenVMS development community. Galaxy, et al, is cool stuff, but where
will OpenVMS be headed once the ISV community--which is responsible for
developing and supporting OVMS apps--realizes that the OS is a
VMSasaurus whose cost outweighs its benefits. It�s not so much a matter
of "where do you want to go today?," but "where will you be *forced* to
go tomorrow?"

There. I've said my piece.

Cheers,

Terry Shannon
Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Compaq
