In article <36843058.415BB923@cyberdude.com>, Dani Steinmetz <danis@cyberdude.com> writes:
:I'm new to OpenVms and Vax enviroment and I need some help with Indexed
:files.

  In general, they're easier than they look.  They're a whole lot 
  easier than the first look at the (admittedly quite intimidating) 
  RMS reference manuals might initially make them out to be, too...

  You are responsible for the organization of the records stored
  in the file, and for defining (using EDIT/FDL or similar) the
  file and the key structure.  You then write records to the file,
  and RMS takes care of tracking the keys.  When you want to read 
  records, you can specify a particular target key or a particular
  starting key, and you can specify sequential access by any key.

:If anyone has examples of indexed file processing that they can share, I
:would greatly appreciate it. I'm coding in Fortran so that would be
:best.  but I'll take anything if it's commented.

  Using some simple DCL procedures calling commands such as OPEN, 
  READ, READ/KEY, WRITE, CLOSE, and CREATE/FDL might well be the 
  easiest approach to learning more about the basic operation of
  indexed files.  You can use EDIT/FDL to interactively create or
  alter the file definition language (FDL) file required by the 
  CREATE/FDL command, and you can use ANALYZE/RMS/FDL to generate 
  an FDL file based on the specified (existing) file.  (There are
  callable routines -- such as fdl$create -- that allow a program 
  to perform similar operations.)

  For examples of indexed file access, check the OpenVMS Freeware. 
  Also see the Guide to OpenVMS File Applications in the documentation 
  set for background information.  Both the first and second editions 
  of the _Guide to Writing Real Programs in DCL_ book (available from
  http://www.bh.com) have examples and related text, there were various 
  updates made in the file-related chapters for the second edition.

  If your site has access to DSNlink, consider looking around online
  for various source code examples -- I'd fully expect a number of 
  Fortran examples of accessing indexed files can be found in the 
  DSNlink support databases.

  You may well find a few examples using the http://www.dejanews.com/ 
  or INFO-VAX newsgroup archives, as well.

