glew@cs.wisc.edu writes:

>> 
>> Although it should also be noted that, for matrix multiply,
>> the TLB misses can be done out-of-order, and could be made
>> to overlap with each other.
>> 
>> The high cost of TLB misses is in large part due to the "stop the world"
>> implementation. P6 allowed TLB misses to proceed without blocking
>> independent instructions.  Pipelining TLB misses is an obvious next step.
>> 

I agree. Nowadays almost all processor implement out of order
execution, and it must be easy to make the LOAD as speculative.
Then we can use non-blocking and/or pipelining to access TLB misses.
Of course without a large L2 TLB it would be useless.

I am thinking to implement L2 TLB for Linux/Alpha palcode, but
many alpha implementation does not allow non-blocking TLB misses
and it will help little for large problems.

