In article <19981229145834.00806.00003575@ng-fv1.aol.com>, captmac44@aol.com (CaptMac44) writes:
:I have a 3/4" blank border around my screen.  I can't seem to get the desktop
:or any file to fill the entire screen.  Any suggestions?

  Terse questions beget terse answers.

  Given that there are a large number of different HiNote systems, you 
  might want to mention the specific model.  The operating system, the
  version, and ECO/SP level might be interesting, too.  You will also 
  want to indicate if this is a new behaviour, and if so, what changed.
  You will also want to check the screen settings in the desktop and 
  (if applicable) in the BIOS.  And you will want to indicate if this 
  occurs in an application or if this occurs with the desktop.  (I need 
  to rummage around and find out if Linux runs on the HiNote, but I 
  digress.  Use of "desktop" assumes the use of Microsoft Windows NT,
  Windows 95, or Windows 98.)

  The pixels on LCD panels don't "stretch", so having an incorrect 
  setting for the screen size can cause blank borders of varying widths
  around the edge of the LCD panel.

  I've seen blank borders around a Broderbund (sp?) Myst display on the 
  HiNote VP 520, for instance.  This is entirely normal -- Myst picked 
  a screen size (and missed), and the VP 520 does the best it can to 
  display it.  (While ex-Myst, a rather larger area of the LCD panel 
  is used.)

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
