In article <Jrbi2.423$xq4.459@news.rdc1.ne.home.com>, "Robert Miller" <rgmiller@home.com> writes:
:    I have ordered a dec alphastation 200/233MHZ for an auction and need to
:find out what harddrive to purchase for it. Dec's page says it has a Fast
:SCSI2 connection. Will a ultra scsi2 or fast wide scsi2 harddrive work, Also
:how many pins on the connector.

  Most SCSI disk drives can be gotten to work with this system, though 
  unless you are familiar with (and willing to deal with) SCSI integration 
  and testing, I'd tend to point you to one of the RZ-series disks.  
  Externally, one of the StorageWorks "pizzaboxes" -- BA353 et al -- and 
  one or more of the RZxxx-VA disks will make a nice combination for this
  system, and with minimal hassles.  (-VA is a StorageWorks narrow brick,
  BA353 is a StorageWorks narrow shelf.)

  UltraSCSI disks will generally "dumb down" to SCSI-2, but I would not
  always count on it -- there are "corner-case" SCSI configurations that
  may not work.  (Hmmm.  Thinking about it, all of SCSI is arguably one 
  big corner-case.  But I digress. :-)  You can't use the UltraSCSI 
  capabilities with this system, so unless you've got the disks kicking 
  around spare...  The AlphaStation 200 SCSI controller was fast-narrow,
  if memory serves.  Newer narrow widgets tend to work on wide, and wide 
  widgets usually work on narrow SCSI.  (This is SCSI, after all, and it 
  all only usually works anyway. :-)

  Most of the used-equipment resellers around can help you with the right 
  part numbers for an internal mounting of various widgets, since I am 
  assuming you don't want to purchase a new disk for this system...

  Typical narrow SCSI disk parts for internal mounting were:

    PBXRZ-BB, -EB, -ED, -HD, -JB, -NA
      (0.535GB, 1.05GB, 1.05GB, 2.1GB, 2.1GB, 4.3GB, respectively)
    PBXRD-CA was the internal CD-ROM
    PBXRX-AA was the interal floppy

  Wide SCSI storage widgets were typically not supported on the standard 
  SCSI controller.

  DWZZB was an external widget that converted narrow to wide SCSI.

  KZPAA-AA FNSE SCSI adapter, and KZPSA-BB was the FWSE adapter.

