Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.benchmarks,comp.sys.hp,comp.sys.dec

Hi,

Dr. Naohiko Shimizu recently proposed a way to organize the computation
of matrix products so that the number of TLB missed was minimized.
TLB misses may occur, for instance, when accessing the elements of
a large matrix row by row, and the number of columns are greater than
the available number of TLB entries. This is the case when calculating
a matrix product using dot products:

c(i,j) = sum_k a(i,k)*b(k,j)   (inner loop over index k)

Accessing elements row-by-row also has other problems, like cache
trashing (cache collisions between elements in the same row.)

For computations with a high degree of data reuse, like BLAS 3, I would
like to point out that data copying techniques can be very useful for 
making better use of the cache and TLB. In the matrix multiply case, the
idea is to copy blocks of A to a work array that fits in the cache, and
at the same time transpose it. Then the inner loop above accesses both A
and B elements column-wise. Compared with Dr. Shimizu's approach, this
way of organizing the loops avoids store instructions in the inner loop.
For processors with write-through L1 caches like SUN's ultrasparc,
reducing the number of store instructions is often a good idea.

Most processors have at least two levels of cache (IBM's power 2 chips
and HP's processors are notable exceptions). These benefit from a
two-level cache blocking strategy: A is blocked of L2 cache and B is
blocked for L1 cache. The matrix multiply code below illustrates this
point. The performance of the code is excellent: for a 1000*1000 matrix
multiply I have measured 320 Mflops (80 % of peak) on a O2000 server
(195 MHz R10000, 4 MB L2 cache) and 304 Mflops (76 % of peak) on a SUN
ultra-2 workstation (200 MHz ultrasparc-I, 1 MB L2 cache.)

In order to raise the flops/cycle ratio above one, outer loop unrolling,
controlled by the directives below, is employed. It is straightforward
(but tedious) to unroll the loops manually, in case the compiler cannot
do it automatically (most don't). The goal of the unrolling technique
(also called register blocking) is to calculate eight elements of the C
matrix at the same time in the inner loop. Then the number of loads
during one iteration is 6 (four from atemp and two from btemp), while
the number of floating point ops is 16. It is not necessary to unroll
the *inner* loop manually, because it will be pipelined by the compiler
(most commercial fortran compilers will do that at a sufficiently high
level of optimization.)


c 'N','N' version of dgemm, alpha=beta=1 assumed for simplicity
c Lennart Bengtsson, Chalmers
c Covered by the GNU public license: free to use for non-commercial
c purposes

      subroutine dgemm_nn(m,n,k,a,b,c)
      integer m,n,k
      real*8 a(m,k),b(k,n),c(m,n)

      integer kb,ib,jb
c Cache blocking parameters
c atemp should fit in L2 cache
c btemp + four columnms of atemp should fit in L1 cache
      parameter(kb=80,ib=1500,jb=16)
      real*8 atemp(kb,ib), btemp(kb,jb), c1
      integer i1,j1,k1,i2,j2,nbi,nbj,nbk

      do k1 = 1, k, kb
         nbk = min(kb,k-k1+1)
         do i1 = 1, m, ib
            nbi = min(ib,m-i1+1)
c Transpose block of A
            do k2 = 1, nbk
               do i = 1, nbi
                  atemp(k2,i) = a(i+i1-1,k2+k1-1)
               enddo
            enddo
            do j1 = 1, n, jb
               nbj = min(jb, n-j1+1)
c Copy block of B to L1 cache
               do j2 = 1, nbj
                  do k2 = 1, nbk
                     btemp(k2,j2) = b(k2+k1-1,j2+j1-1)
                  enddo
               enddo
C*$* UNROLL (4)
               do i2 = 1, nbi
C*$* UNROLL (2)
                  do j2 = 1, nbj
                     c1 = c(i1+i2-1,j2+j1-1)
c dot product
                     do k2 = 1, nbk
                        c1 = c1 + atemp(k2,i2)*btemp(k2,j2)
                     enddo
                     c(i1+i2-1,j2+j1-1) = c1
                  enddo
               enddo
            enddo
         enddo
      enddo
      end



Lennart Bengtsson
Department of Applied Physics
Chalmers University of Technology
Sweden

