In article <367C78EF.D68F7567@nospam.iastate.edu>, Frank Samuelson <fwsam@nospam.iastate.edu> writes:
>From: Frank Samuelson <fwsam@nospam.iastate.edu>
>Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
>Subject: mouting images
>
>I have an iso-9660 CD image on my SCSI drive.  I would like to be 
>able to mount and read this image as a file system.

The mount command you want is something like:

# mount -t cdfs -r block-device mount-point

To get rid of version numbers and map uppercase to lowercase
use the option "-o noversion".  If the image has Rock Ridge
extension use "-o noversion,rrip".  Since you're running V4.0D
you do not need to rebuild the kernel; the driver for the file
system is dynamically loadable.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




