References: <75roff$746$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: GEC Marconi Materials Technology
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

kseshadri@hns.com wrote:
> 
> while trying to bridge decnet traffic across multiple routers I
> notice that the hardware address changes depending upon the router
> segment I sniff on. Can some one point me to a paper or website that
> explains how and why this happens ?
> 

If I understand the question correctly, what you are seeing is the way 
Decnet Phase IV avoids the need for routers on a LAN.

What happens is that the address of the ethernet adapter is changed to
a new value defined as AA-00-04-00-yz-wx where wxyz is the HEX
equivalent
of the Decnet address (note the byte reversal).

In other words, to turn an address like 43.7 into an Ethernet address:

1	Calculate 43*1024+7
2	Show it in HEX
3	Swap the first and second bytes
4	The ethernet address is AA-00-04-00- followed by these two bytes

This means that any decnet host on a LAN can address any other without
needing
a router.

In the case of a real router, the two ethernet addresses will be
different
because the two interfaces have different Decnet addresses.

Note that systems using Phase V decnet usually also have Phase IV
addresses.
It is possible to configure systems to use ONLY Phase V routing, and
then the
above behaviour doesn't happen. However, they will only be able to
communicate
with Phase V systems (and other OSI systems).

-- 
Alan Adams
Marconi



