system@SendSpamHere.ORG (Brian Schenkenberger, VAXman-) wrote:

>In article <914663934.528634@terra.ultra.net.au>, phaedrus@ultra.net.au (Darryl Georgee) writes:
>>Have a TK50 expansion box with a uVax2000.
>>On the back of the Tk50 box are 3 dip switches.
>>Can anyone suggest what they should be set at?
>>Also I tried to format a RD52A on the uVax2000
>>However the format programme didnt seem to recognise
>>the RD52A and started asking me lotz of questions re the 
>>drive which I couldnt answer. Is this normal?
>>Thankx, Darryl Georgee, Townsville, Australia.

>It would appear then that you have a TK50Z-GA.  The original TK50 expansion
>for the uV2K was a TK50Z-FA.  It looks very much like the *-GA with excep-
>tion of the SCSI address dip switches and a firmware revision difference.

>If you are running VMS on the uV2K, the driver (TVDRIVER) expects the CPU
>to have SCSI id 0 and the tape driver to be address at SCSI id 1.  Simple
>set the switches correspond to this value.

>You can test that you have set the switches correctly from the uV2K's con-
>sole. Booted up without a tape driver, test ">>> T 50" should return the
>following:

>[...]
>TPC      0000.4001
>   FFFFFF03 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 
>[...]

>   ^-id 0-^ ^-id 1-^ ^-id 2-^ ^-id 3-^ ^-id 4-^ ^-id 5-^ ^-id 6-^ ^-id 7-^

>Install the TK50 drive and issue the console command ">>> T 6" and then,
>issue the ">>> T 50" command to display the status.

>With the TK50 installed and *properly* addressed, the output should look
>like the following:

>[...]
>TPC      0202.0001
>   FFFFFF03 01000001 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 
>[...]

>   ^-id 0-^ ^-id 1-^ ^-id 2-^ ^-id 3-^ ^-id 4-^ ^-id 5-^ ^-id 6-^ ^-id 7-^


>If any other field appears as "01000001", you have the addressing wrong.
>Reset the switches and try again.

>--
>VAXman- OpenVMS APE certification number: AAA-0001          VAXman@TMESIS.COM

