David Mason Curry <dcurry@silo.csci.unt.edu> writes:
> Are SCSIA and SCSIB really just the e same thing, or are there two SCSI
> controllers in a VAXstation 3100 (m38 for me)?  I am confused because
> the documention indicates that I can only have 7 SCSI devices when I
> expected to be able to connect 14 with two seperate buses.

  There are a variety of different SCSI controllers in those systems. The
most common are a hybrid SCSI/RQDX version which supports internal RD/RX
drives and external SCSI (found on older systems) and the dual SCSI which
supports internal and external devices on two buses. There's also a sin-
gle SCSI w/o RQDX and a floppy-only (no SCSI, no HD) RQDX. Some of these
may have been CSS-ish options for specific customers.

  As I recall (it's been a few years) one SCSI bus is internal only, and
runs around the inside of the box in an off-white round cable with black
connectors. Both ends of this cable go to the same connector (different
pins) on the SCSI controller daughterboard, which has 2 sets of terminators
(one for each logical end of the bus). A second SCSI bus loops out of the
daugherboard on a ribbon cable to a single device position (behind the
removable front panel faceplate) and is used for a SCSI floppy or tape
drive. This cable also uses the 2-ends-on-one-connector, but there's only
one set of terminators - the other end of the bus comes out a bass-ackwards
68-pin connector that looks like wide SCSI, but isn't. There's a snap-on
68-pin terminator under a plastic cover on the back of the case. If you
remove the cover and terminator, that's where external connectors attach.
I think the cable is a BC56H, but don't hold me to that.

  Each bus can support 7 SCSI ID's (and the controllers are normally at ID
6, not the more common ID 7). The M30 and M38 are limited by only having
4 mounting positions, but the M40 and M48 (which use the same controller)
have an extra shelf.

  Both the console firmware and VMS support LUNs (though older VMS versions
require you to manually attach the LUNs, so the boot disk should be the
only LUN on an ID, and at LUN 0 on that ID). So you can have more disks
than you'd ever need (over 50) if you buy some LUN expanders. However, you
should note that the M3x and M4x SCSI controllers are async SCSI and only
do 1.5Mbytes/sec peak, so they're not exactly good performers.

  Due to a lack of interest from DEC in supporting older boxes, the console
firmware only understands 6-byte SCSI commands, so any bootable disk must
be smaller than 1.<small number> GB unless you want to do funny tricks to
make sure it's bootable.

