This is a very subjective decision in some cases and an extremely
objective decision in other cases.

1. If it ain't broke, don't fix it, is generally followed by many folks in
the business.

2. As you update the Operating System on your Alpha, you should also be
updating the firmware on your Alpha. The firmware release notes will
provide you with information that assist you in making this decision in
many cases.


As a general rule of thumb, all Alpha Firmware relases contain enhanced
support for new options. That's the way it goes. You introduce a new
option, you have to provide support for it in the firmware. New options
are being released all the time. As a second general rule of thumb, All
ALpha Frimware releases contain bug fixes. There may be latent support
contained within a release for a specific piece of hardware, but when the
hardware is officially released, the support for it may be enhanced. You
may observer this from time to time as interim releases that fall between
the quarterly release of the Alpha Firmware Update CD-ROM currently at
version 5.3 New funstionality always finds it's way into new releases.

I, in my capacity as a system manager, generally review and update the
firmware in lockstep with the firmware cd-rom, and always update in
conjunction with new hardware support and new versions of the Operating
Systems.

I would strongly encourage everyone to read the release notes and make a
determination for themselves.

rtt

