I just looked at the response I sent.  I did a typo.  The line in
/etc/securettys should be ptys (not ttys).  This allows root logins
over the network.  One word of warning, this is not secure.  Generally
it is safer to add the user to /etc/group, then they can log in as
themselves over the network and do an su for root.

Rocky

Rocky wrote:
> 
> Leon,
> 
> To give a user the ability to su to root add their login name to the
> system line in the /etc/group file.  To allow root logins over the
> network add the line
> 
> ttys
> 
> as the last line in the /etc/securettys file.
> 
> Regards,
> 
> Rocky
>



