Organization: Compaq UNIX System Division
Reply-To: aaron@zk3.dec.com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


You'll find this in the C language standard ISO/IEC 9899:1990(E) section
	7.9.6.  Formatted input/output functions

Here's the example, expanded a bit:

% cat foo.c        
void main (void)
{
   long dummy = 18446471394825863167; /* from bc:  2^64-2^48+2^43-1  */
              /*   FFFF07FFFFFFFFFF      from bc: obase=16 ; 2^64-2^48+2^43-1 */

   printf("Dummy is %#0 24ld (  signed dec via '%%#0 24ld')\n", dummy);
   printf("Dummy is %#0 24li (  signed dec via '%%#0 24li')\n", dummy);
   printf("Dummy is %#0 24lu (unsigned dec via '%%#0 24lu')\n", (unsigned long)dummy);
   printf("Dummy is %#0 24lo (unsigned oct via '%%#0 24lo')\n", (unsigned long)dummy);
   printf("Dummy is %#0 24lx (unsigned hex via '%%#0 24lx')\n", (unsigned long)dummy);
}
% cc -O -o foo{,.c}
% ./foo            
Dummy is -00000000272678883688449 (  signed dec via '%#0 24ld')
Dummy is -00000000272678883688449 (  signed dec via '%#0 24li')
Dummy is 000018446471394825863167 (unsigned dec via '%#0 24lu')
Dummy is 001777770177777777777777 (unsigned oct via '%#0 24lo')
Dummy is 0x000000ffff07ffffffffff (unsigned hex via '%#0 24lx')

Hope this helps you.
=Aaron

