Sue McCartin <SMccartin@csi.com> writes...
}I have this machine at work, it has windows NT 4.0 on it.  I am a
}computer guru (predominately hardware) but I don't know a lot about NT,
}I'm used to 95 only.  In short, our tech seems to be having a helluva
}time getting the video driver to install.  It is a stock box with
}embedded everything.  I've heard three different versions of what the
}video chip is supposed to be (Fire GL, S3 Virge, and Matrox Mystique)
}and the drivers that came on the cdrom do not work apparently.  I
}watched him mess with it, it simply doesn't see anything but a stock vga
}controller and removing the old one results in the same driver being
}added on next boot up.  Such a nice machine to use but it's awful to
}look at in 16 colors--what a waste.  In addition to the video chip
}refusing to setup it seems to also refuse to recognize the embedded
}sound chip--NT says there is no sound hardware but according to the
}documentation I've seen there is an embedded sound chip and there is a
}speaker output on the back of the case.  I see a couple of bios updates
}in the driver section of digital's site, can anyone speak on why NT
}isn't seeing this hardware or whether it is a matter of a bios update?
}All assistance is greatfully appreciated.   Sue.

Based on info from the Digital web site (see
www.windows.digital.com/products/Desktop/digitalpc_5510_abstract.asp)...

The "Competative Comparison" chart claims that it has a "Matrox MGA 1164SG
(PCI)" as standard graphics.

The MGA 1164SG chip is used in the Matrox Mystique 220 (and the older just
plain "Mystique").

So my guess would be the Mystique. The NT drivers for this at www.matrox.com
work fine for me.

I don't know what the sound is, but it is supposed to be soundblaster
compatible. A real soundblaster card is an option, so I expect that this
is not really one.

You might pop open your case and look at the sound chip to see what
it is - it's probably marked with at least the manufacturer. While you're
in there, check the graphics chip to see if it is the Matrox chip.

Perhaps even they do not know what is in the thing, which is why the don't
say what it is in their techincal specs.

If the system came with NT installed it should have already had all the
drivers (and they should be on the CD it came with too), so I am guessing
that it came with 95 and was upgraded to NT after it got there.  In such
a case the graphics, and possibly the sound, are probably just newer than
the NT CD you are using so the proper drivers are not on it (I've had this
problem before), although the Mystique is pretty old so it should be on there
but it might not be identifying itself as the same thing as a real Mystique.

--- Carl



