Chris wrote:
> 
> andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com wrote:
> 
> > The features such as hot-plug, hot-upgrade,
> > etc which are also available on Suns do
> > not exist on VAXs or Alphas unless you have
> > them clustered using OpenVMS and then only
> > if you are prepared to shut down the node
> > you want to tamper with.
> >
> 
> Ok, the hot swap vs cluster arguement reflects a difference in approach
> to the solution of the same problem. Much of this is arguement about
> semantics - hot swap turns a cpu off, clustering shuts down a node
> (cpu). At user level, there is a reduction in overall system
> performance, in one case, a couple of minutes, in the other perhaps 4 or
> 5 minutes. Hardly relevant.
> 

No its entirely different. Sun can also cluster multiple 
nodes together and then you could shut an indevidual 
node down.

Think of the scenario when you only have one node and 
no cluster, how does you shut the whole node down replace
a component help systems availability.

Or think of a scenario where you have two 10 CPU 8400s 
clustered using OpenVMS, as a couple of customers I work with do. 
Are you going to suggest that taking out 10 of your 20 CPUs 
is anything like say taking out 2 of 20 on a Sun.

Or taking down a whole node to replace a PSU or FAN, something 
that has no impact on a Sun at all. 
 
> In terms of system resilience however, there are more significant
> differences -

> The cluster method will always be a more resilient model than hot swap
> modules on a single backplane. You could literally pull the power plug
> on a cluster member without pulling the rest of the system down. But in
> the case of the hot swap backplane, what happens if one of the tristate
> buffers or gate arrays used for module isolation goes down with one or
> more outputs hard logic low or high ?. One or more bus lines becomes
> stuck and the whole system is pulled down.
> 

I agree that clustering removes some of the single 
points of failure, no one would argue otherwise. 

But if you want HA clustering you can have this with 
UNIX, the point is that the Sun UNIX nodes have much 
better resiliance than OpenVMS nodes. 

Finally clustering may not be an option for many 
customers. This may be a suprise but even Oracle 
do not support Oracle applications (financials etc) 
on top of a parallel cluster. 

> The clustering model is elegant in that the 'module isolation' can be
> implemented as a software driver layer. What common hardware there is
> can be made very reliable because it can be implemented using only
> simple passive components like fibre optic cable, coax ethernet etc.
> 

It is but having highly reliable indevidual nodes is 
also highly desirable, particulary if you cannot run 
your application across multiple nodes because the 
app does not support it or if you only have one node.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



