  I see layers of speculation layered on more speculation, leading to
  a degree of certainty that may or may not be warranted.

  "Decent I/O subsystem and 12 CPUs" is also a matter of opinion -- I
  am sure the E4500 has what Sun perceives as a reasonable tradeoff in
  cost and performance -- the hallmark of most system designs -- but
  without a whole lot more details, comparisions among these are often
  futile.  As has been discussed repeatedly here, the "decent I/O subsystem 
  and 12 CPUs" may or may not effect the resulting performance of the 
  application, which (all marketing smoke -- from any source -- aside) is 
  the bottom line for any customer.

:The next issue is finding an application that may 
:gain advantage by having a large SGA.

  "SGA" is not a term used on OpenVMS -- if you are marketing to OpenVMS
  customers, please translate your terms: virtual address space is the
  term used on OpenVMS, and there are 63 bits of (flat) virtual address 
  space available for user processes among the P0, P1 and P2 regions,
  and an equivilent amount of (flat) address space in S0, S1, and S2
  system regions.

:Datawarehouses for example do not generally fall into this  category.

  An interesting conclusion, as there are various database vendors 
  looking to use or using the larger address space available.

