Jan Vorbrueggen wrote:
> 
> "andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com" <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com> writes:
> 
> > Being able to configure say 12 CPUs and 8GB of
> > RAM and a decent I/O subsystem is hardly going
> > to cut it since this isn't going to allow you
> > much more than 4 GB of SGA say for TPC-C as an example.
> 
> Well, the benhcmarks were run on the 10-cpu nodes. IIRC. That's five CPU
> boards. Then you can have up to 12 GB of memory. And why would you expect
> that with an 8 GB system, only half of that would go to the SGA? Are you
> saying the OS and the application are taking 4 GB of storage? Even with
> post-modern bloat I don't believe that.
> 
>         Jan


The GS140/8400s Memory/CPU/IO tradeoffs are pretty obvious from 
the Digital benchmark results.

The GS140 TPC-C benchmark used 8 CPUs and 16 GB of RAM.
Digitals benchmarking folks were clearly having to balance 
the requirements for having a large SGA, with the need 
to have as much CPU resource as possible. 

The GS140 TPC-D result used 10 CPUs and 8 GB of RAM this 
was probably because there is less benefit from having 
more RAM for TPC-D and because the additional slot was 
required for I/O.

Neither Sun or HP have to make this kind of tradeoff
for their largest systems. 

BTW I am not suggesting that the OS takes up 4 GB, but 
that OS, buffers, TP monitor, apps, etc eat into your 
available memory.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



