Andrew, I still have not heard you answer some of my questions,
  and please don't take them as bashing your product (my biggest
  disagreement with you is your notes in the VMS newsgroup, where
  you can't overcome the largest product deficit - it isn't VMS -
  and no amount of wiz-bang features are going to make you welcome
  in that group).

  - Please tell me what non-catestrophic CPU failure modes can
    be detected that would allow a user to hot-swap a "failing"
    CPU?  What are the min/max time to shut down a CPU?  Can you
    shut down any CPU without restrictions?

  - Can components be removed?  As opposed to a quiece, replace,
    restart?  Memory?  IO?  CPUs?  Does a hot-swap of say, memory,
    require it's replacement - or can it be out-swapped?

  - How do you shutdown a non-responsive CPU (either hung, or
    catastrophically failed)?

  - How do you ride through a controller fault and have confidence
    in the integrity of the system?  What device controller failures
    do you see that actually require the replacement of the actual
    controller?  Most non-catestrophic controller errors in my
    experience are due to faulty external connections (disks, net,
    etc).  What controllers does hot-swap make sense for?  Is the
    real feature the ability to remove and upgrade to different
    faster/better controllers?

  - How can you tell that a failing disk controller hasn't corrupted
    the disk or memory?

  - How do you ride through a uncorrectable multi-bit memory error?

  - How do you ride though, say, a uncorrectable error on the
    address data for a write flowing through your crossbar?

  - How do you ride though a software fault in the O/S or driver?

  - If you inswap new devices, how are they named?  Is the naming
    that occurs via the inswap persistant?  That is, if you did a
    reboot of the system, would the naming be the same?



