Would you care to make a more accurate estimate 
of how much available space you would have for 
a SGA on a 6 GB 8400 being used for say TPC-C. 

>   "Decent I/O subsystem and 12 CPUs" is also a matter of opinion -- I
>   am sure the E4500 has what Sun perceives as a reasonable tradeoff in
>   cost and performance -- the hallmark of most system designs -- but
>   without a whole lot more details, comparisions among these are often
>   futile.  As has been discussed repeatedly here, the "decent I/O subsystem
>   and 12 CPUs" may or may not effect the resulting performance of the
>   application, which (all marketing smoke -- from any source -- aside) is
>   the bottom line for any customer.
> 

Well perhaps I should quantify this. An E4500 with 
12 CPUs and 12 GB of memory would have an I/O subsystem 
with a total bandwidth if you used the remaining 
systems slots for I/O controllers of 2.1 GB/s. The 
backplane all of this plugges into has a bandwith of 
~3.2 GB/s. Sustained memory throughput would be ~2.8 GB/s.

In comparison a GS140 configured with 10 CPUs could 
have a maximum memory of 12 GB it would have a sustained 
memory throughput of ~1.1 GB/s and a maximum I/O 
bandwidth of 500 MB/s.


> :The next issue is finding an application that may
> :gain advantage by having a large SGA.
> 
>   "SGA" is not a term used on OpenVMS -- if you are marketing to OpenVMS
>   customers, please translate your terms: virtual address space is the
>   term used on OpenVMS, and there are 63 bits of (flat) virtual address
>   space available for user processes among the P0, P1 and P2 regions,
>   and an equivilent amount of (flat) address space in S0, S1, and S2
>   system regions.
> 

It is if you run Oracle regardless of the system. The SGA 
is the Oracle Systems Global Area, Oracle uses it as a cache 
among other things.

> :Datawarehouses for example do not generally fall into this  category.
> 
>   An interesting conclusion, as there are various database vendors
>   looking to use or using the larger address space available.
> 

I suggest that you look at the memory sizes used by vendors for 
large Datawarehouse benchmarks and compare this with the memory 
used for OLTP benchmarks such as TPC-C and SAP on the same vendors 
systems, what you find is that the value of having more memory is 
most evident in the OLTP benchmarks and less for data warehousing. 

As an example Digital used 16 GB of memory and 8 CPUs for TPC-C 
and 10 CPUs and 8 GB of memory for each node in their TPC-D 
benchmark, the Digital benchmarking folks obviously see the 
benefits/requirment of more memory for OLTP and more I/O and CPUs 
for DW. 

Given the kind of access patern into the SGA that a DW type 
query does this is not that suprising.

