Lets inject a little reality into you attempted jibe.

At the 30 to 40,000 TPM mark having a <4 GB SGA is not 
a problem it is above that. 

Back in 1996 or was it earlier when Digital started 
marketing the hell out of VLDB64 the maximum single 
system TPC-C TPM's were in the 15,000 to 20,000 mark.

This was because the CPUs, I/O and DBMSs were not capable 
of doing any better.

It is only very recently that a single system has beaten 
the 40,000 mark for TPM making VLDB64 a necessity for
good TPC-C results. 

Incedentally it was HP who beat this mark not Digital 
because they were able to put CPUs memory and I/O 
all into one box at the same time. Note this also 
exposes the Digital VLDB64 charade for what it was.

You can sidestep the >4GB SGA requirements by using 
OPS and multiple Oracle instances because each 
instance now has its own SGA. Note again this 
is exactly what Digital did to escape the 
limmitations imposed by the 8400 with their 
clustered TPC-C TPM numbers. Not in this 
case a software limit but a hardware limit 
imposed by the 8400 design.

You should also note that unlike many other 
OLTP applications TPC-C which is only a benchmark 
is very dependant on SGA size. 

SAP and BaaN both of which are applications benchmarks 
are not SGA dependant and to illustrate this point. 
Sun holds the SAP record with ~3.5x the real throughput 
of an8400 running 64 bit DU with Solaris 2.6 a 32 bit OS.

You also seem very unwilling to introduce any 
real examples of independant DBMS benchamrks run 
on say Digital and another platform that show 
the benefits of VLDB64.

