Paul Chapin wrote: 
> That was interesting but didn't deal with my problem.  I have a file on the
> UNIX box that is owned by another user but has the ACL set up so that I can
> read and write it.  I can verify that I can access it directly on the UNIX.
> I can read the file on my Win98 system and bring it up in Notepad.  But when
> I try to save the file back I'm told that I need a new name since the file
> is read-only.  So why is the ACL read permission being recognized but not
> the write permission.
> 
> This may be a problem with ACLs on dUnix 4.0D since I've had problem with my
> web server not recognizing the execute setting in ACLs for cgi scripts.

What happens when you try to save the edited file to your local machine,
and then copy it over to the share? What happens when you try the same
operation with the share mapped rather than accessed directly?

I've run into instances where I tried editing a file via a share and
could not save the file directly back even though I was the owner of the
file. However, saving the file locally and then copying into place
worked fine. Mapping the drive rather than accessing the share directly
allowed me to save the files without the intermediate copy step. I'm not
sure if this is a Samba, Software, or Win9x thing (I suspect the
latter).

