Chris wrote:
> 
> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> 
> >
> > Well, Suns for example have temperature sensors on major
> > components like CPU's, Systems boards etc. These components
> > have maximum operating temperatures. SyMon (part of Solaris)
> > samples these sensors a gradually rising CPU temperature,
> > coupled with no change in case temperature would cause
> > SyMon to predict that the CPU was approaching failure.
> >
> 
> If the cpu is that faulty and overheated, it's dead for data long before
> any sensor would detect the fact. But, assuming you get a faulty cpu,
> just how does the system detect this and switch it out to allow the
> others to continue ?.
> 

The CPU's can be turned off, Solaris has a command called 
psradm that can do this.

Of course you need to be able to do this before the CPU 
actually fails which is what SyMon attempts to do.

> > Simular for ECC memory errors etc etc.
> 
> I see, so all the memory chips have heat sensors as well...
> 
> Some older OS's and machines (dare I say Vax ?) have had all this for
> years - ecc memory was standard on all Vaxen (Except uVax I ?), as was
> os support to mark bad pages so they didn't get mapped in again. Error
> log reports providing an ongoing picture of cpu, memory, io and network
> states and errors etc etc ad nauseum.
> 

Wow, it may suprise you that ECC has been a available 
for most servers for years. 

Error logs are also hardly a failure analysis product 
they are just one source that a failure analysis product 
would use. 

> So, to sum up, nothing really new here - other than Sun waking up to the
> fact that gracefull system degradation is an important issue.
> 

I am not sure how you drew this conclusion, Solaris 
has always had the facility to turn CPUs off. Sun 
servers going back 10 years have had ECC memory. 

No one has suddenly woken up

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



