On vms, if a multiple bit memory error is detected, the offending page
gets marked bad so it's not used again by the os, with an entry made
into the error log. Only the offending page gets disabled though, the
remainder of the memory is still available for use. Single bit errors
also get an error log entry, but are corrected by the ecc. You don't
need to do anything as crude as pull a circuit board till you're good
and ready.

As for removing a simm from a live circuit board, tell me another. Why
would you want to ?. In the real world, field engineers may hot swap
whole memory boards, but the idea of hot swapping a single simm from a
_live_ board on a running system is laughable...

> a failing simm module before it fails... 

So we have precognition now as well ?...

Rgrds, Chris



