Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:

> Nice try but why did you use an old Sun result. Lets put this
> in perspective Sun now has a number of obver 27,000 Qppd on a
> 64 way E10K this makes the 40 CPU's used on the GS140 to get
> 1/3 of the throughput pretty stupid.

[QppD is no throughput number, just in case. Compare QthD and it already looks a
little bit different].
But as some other poster already told you and as TPC strongly
recommends: Do not compare results of different databases.
Reasons should be obvious. So above comparison of a Oracle8i
vs. Informix XPS benchmark is meaningless and of no value.
So much said, on a first glimpse the new Sun 1TB result does
look quite good. Unfortunately there is currently no other Oracle 8i
result that would allow to do a real comparison.
However, looking a bit more detailed at Sun's new 1TB result
reveals a couple of interesting details:

Here are the query and update times (in seconds) for the two recent Sun UE10k TPC-D
tests (Oracle) with an improvement factor to show where the performance gains are:

Sun UE1000 11/3   Sun UE10000 4/18 Improvement
Oracle 8.1.5      Oracle 8.0.4      Factor
Q1    1.9         Q1    3616.1      1903.2x
Q2a   73.1        Q2a   191.3       2.6x
Q3    75.9        Q3    679.6       9.0x
Q4    113.6       Q4    567.3       5.0x
Q5    71.5        Q5    1315.5      18.4x
Q6    71.7        Q6    80          1.1x
......
......
and so on....

The average improvement factor is 3.3x from the 4/18 result to the 11/3 result.
What significantly stands out in the Q1 improvement of 1903x!  Q1 is generally the
longest running query, so a speed up of this magnitude has a strong affect on the
over power metric (3.0x).  Throughput does not benefit nearly as much (1.6x).

To achieve this, Oracle has implemented features that offload work from the queries
to the build time.  This can be seen by looking at the database load times:

Sun UE1000 11/3   Sun UE10000 4/18   Additional time
Oracle 8.1.5      Oracle 8.0.4       needed for load
44hrs 35min       27hrs 22min 2sec   17hrs 13min

These Oracle features include Materialized Views. These are aggregate tables that
are used transparently (i.e. without changing query text, just like indexes) to
save the work of calculating commonly used expressions.  Although they make many
queries go very fast, they need to be maintained during updates:

Sun UE1000 11/3   Sun UE10000 4/18  Additional time
Oracle 8.1.5      Oracle 8.0.4      for updates
UF1   3302.4      UF1   1966.5      1335.9
UF2   4339.1      UF2   3267        1072.1

In addition to more time needed for updates, a larger amount of storage space is
needed to contain the materialized views.  This can be demonstrated by comparing
the database size for the two implementations:

Sun UE1000 11/3   Sun UE10000 4/18  Additional
Oracle 8.1.5      Oracle 8.0.4      storage needed
9.87TB            5.59TB            4.42TB


Conclusion

At first glance, the Sun UE1000 result of 27024.55QppD is quite impressive.  But
the performance is really attributed to an improved Q1 time.  The power metric
benefits significantly since it's based on a geometric mean of the query times.
The through metric is benefited to a much lesser extent since it is based on an
arithmetic mean (average) of query times.  This overall benefit is not for free.
The work must be done during the database load and on a much larger I/O
configuration.

It will be interesting to see the gains of other platforms
using the possibilities of  8i.

And just a final word on TPC-D. Around March 99, TPC-D specification V2 will come
into effect. Among various things
it will get rid of the QppD metric and mainly base on the
QphD model and it will require multi-stream runs for the
queries, for example a 1TB test will require 7 parallel streams.
Sun's current 1TB result did not do (as most other vendors)
multi-stream.

>
>
> Sun does over 2x the TPC-D per CPU than a GS140
> despite having on paper CPU's that are half as fast.

Oh dear... And looking at TPC-C per CPU suddenly
things change again. Let's look at your latest
ue6500/sybase result:
53'049 /24 = 2210 tpmC/CPU
compared to
37'540 /8 = 4693 tpmC/CPU for Compaq.
And it's even the same database (Sybase 11.9).
so ?

