As some people writing here actually _understand_, that is a basic
attribute of computing.  That is why the Sun SMP system does not
gain as much from adding CPU #62 as it gained from adding CPU #4.
Coordinating the operation of multiple CPUs in a single instance
of the operating system gets tougher with more CPUs.

Now if the goal is to measure the ability of operating systems
to handle this, one should measure the relative increase in
performance with the addition of the Nth CPU.  You can measure
Sun's 14th CPU against VMS's 14th CPU.

If you disagree that software slowdowns contribute to SMP performance
scaling issue, then presumably you would attribute the drop-off on the
Sun box to bus problems, but your colleague Andrew Harrison has praised
the Sun bus technology to high heavens.  It would be a shame to have
this comp.os.vms discussion turn into an argument between two Sun folk
regarding where their bottleneck is.  You _do_ believe that every
computing system has a bottleneck, don't you ?  Even a system that is
"balanced" for a particular application, measured close enough...

