Hoff Hoffman <hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam> wrote:
>
>
>Steve Bellenot wrote:
>
>: While there are many bottlenecks in any computer system, none of them
>: are about "Amdahl law". Amdahl law does not predict that there is any
>: wall. It meerly points out that if for example 10% of your code is
>: sequential, then you can never get a speed-up of more than 10.
>: 
>: Amdahl law does not apply to many systems. For example running multiple
>: copies of the same job, is essentially all parallel.
>
>  Think holisticly -- consider the entire system.  If it were as simple 
>  as running another job, much of the plague of parallel processing 
>  paradigm problems would have disappeared into a puff of greasy orange 
>  smoke long ago.

When did holisticly become to mean "by analogy"? Amdahl law is about
theory and not about practice. The embarrassingly parallel example is
not about practice either, although there are many things that come
close.

>  There are very few application systems that have no critical path 
>  somewhere: as examples, consider applications that process a database 
>  in parallel, or a series of applications that all write to seperate 
>  disks.  Eventually, you will encounter a critical path -- it could be 
>  in the I/O subsystem, in the database, or somewhere else. But eventually, 
>  you encounter some task that you just can't perform in parallel fast 
>  enough, and you hit the wall...

Every system has a critical path. Critical paths are about minimal
completion time and can have little to say about parallelism. Indeed
a prefectly 100 way parallel program would have (in theory) 100 
(or more) different critical paths. (Although in practice, it is more
likely they all differ slightly in length, so it is more common to
have many fewer critical paths.)

(Insert witty comment about greasy orange smoke.)
