Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.Com (Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer) writes:


> 
> This is, of course, only partially true.  It allows you to side step software
> bottlenecks, it doesn't allow you to sidestep hardware bottlenecks.
> 
Actually, in a way it does.  One of the major issues with an SMP
system(be it bus-based, crossbar, partially connected, snoop CC, or
directory CC) is that as the number of processors increases the
overhead for maintaining cache coherence will go up.  This is a simple
fact of ALL CCSMP designs.  Done properly, what something like galaxy
will allow you to trade off CC overhead for other overheads.  The goal
is that because you have more knowledge of what specifically you are
doing, you can minimize the sync overhead.

Its the basic issue of who has more knowledge, the application writer
or the hardware engineer/architect?  


=
> If you can make a task run faster by splitting the OS in two instances
> on teh same hardware, this really only means one thing: the scalability of
> the OS sucks.  In other words: there is a software bottleneck in the OS
> that limits the number of processors that can be used effectively
> for a certain task.  For some reason, such a task can be split up in multple
> independent tasks in such a way that, even though more communication outside
> the single system image is needed, that extra communication is cheaper
> than living with the bottleneck.  It must be quite a bottleneck then.


Refer to my comments above.  The best hardware and software designed
will not scale for some workloads because of coherence/sync issues
that the hardware must maintain to be general purpose.  There are
applications that actually scale better past a certain number of CPU's
through message passing(NCC-SMP) than if they were written as CCSMP
applications.



