> Temperature sensor have recently become more popular due to:
> 
> a) Availability of cheap sensor technology
> 
> b) Use as a value-added PC sales.  "Our PC clones are better than your
> PC clone because ours has a temperature sensor - Nah, Nah, Nah!"
> 
> IMHO, the value of temperature sensors are vastly over rated.

The biggest problem is non- or limited redudancy, coupled with a failure rate
for the sensors that is usually higher, and often much higher, than the
failure rate of the system being monitored. I remember at CERN that a VAX8650,
at the time one of CERN's main time-sharing systems, shutting down repeatedly
because of a sensor indicating a fan failure...when that fan was happily
blowing along. Another example: NASA has had a number of shutdowns of Space 
Shuttle main engines on the pad, and even one in flight, because of sensor
failures. In the latter case, they actually inhibited the automatic shutdown
on further engines after the first failure, and this real-time decision is
credited with a near-normal flight (abort-to-orbit) instead of an immedaite
landing which would have had much higher risk to shuttle and crew.

Oh, BTW, there's been such a trending product as Andy describes available from
DEC for VAX/VMS about 10, 15 years ago...was that VAx Performance Advisor?

	Jan



