Steve Bellenot wrote:
> In article <74gvcd$jhv@usenet.pa.dec.com>,
> Hoff Hoffman <hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam> wrote:
> >  As has been discussed recently in the comp.os.vms newsgroup, symmetric
> >  multiprocessing (SMP) can improve aggregate performance, but something
> >  often called Amdahl's Law eventually arises.  Though I am paraphrasing
> >  here, Amdahl has stated that the performance scaling one could expect
> >  from adding processors to an SMP configuration directly corresponds to
> >  the amount of work that could operate in parallel.  In other words, most
> >  any computer environment would eventually encounter a critical path that
> >  effectively limits the number of processors, either in the application
> >  or in the operating system itself.  Adding processors eventually won't
> >  help once the "knee" is reached.
> 
> While there are many bottlenecks in any computer system, none of them
> are about "Amdahl law". Amdahl law does not predict that there is any
> wall. It meerly points out that if for example 10% of your code is
> sequential, then you can never get a speed-up of more than 10.
> 
> Amdahl law does not apply to many systems. For example running multiple
> copies of the same job, is essentially all parallel.

????

Amdahl law states that the relative amount of code, that can not benefit
from
parallel execution, will limit the relative gain by parallel execution.

All computer systems both hardware and software has something that can
not be
executed parallel. So Amdahls law will always apply.

The big question is how big is this effect in practice. 50% => x2 and
0.1% => x1000.

It depends a lot on the problems in question. It also depends a lot on
what kind
of restrictions there are on choice of hardware/os/compilers/application
etc..

Arne



