In article <casper.913115717@nl-usenet.sun.com>, Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.Com (Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer) writes:

>>	Ahh, but they can READ from each other.  Not write to each
>>	other as you conclude it could be nasty.
> 
> So, how does protection work?  Is the MMU hardware shared and is
> it indeed physically impossible for one instance to steal pages
> and destroy them?  And would a CPU failure in one instance kill a whole
> box or just the one instance?

Individual application programs do not control memory management,
only instances of the operating system do that (you have to be in
kernel mode).

Certainly a rogue CPU could undo the careful design of VMS and put
the wrong memory management control signals out, but that would
seem no more likely than a rogue memory management unit doing so
under a different design that had the sharing control entirely
built into the memory management unit.

Individual application programs can _use_ shared memory (Galactic
global section for those who know VMS programming; no offense to those
who do not), but they must ask the operating system nicely to set it
up and they must have the quotas authorized by the system manager for
that activity.

The business about permissions and quotas is nothing special about
Galaxy; it is a natural outgrowth of the traditional VMS management
paridigm.

Larry Kilgallen




