In article <366E73B0.190A48A7@uk.sun.nospam.com>, Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com> writes:
:> So, with Sun you hot swap a new cpu module, with vax you bring another
:> node into the cluster - the net effect is the same - so what are you
:> driving at ?.
:The net effect is not the same. 

  Quite true, the net effect is not the same -- in fact, I'd tend to be
  very disappointed if the effects were exactly the same.

  This is, after all, a comparision of two quite different ways to solve
  the same problem -- citations of the liabilities of one approach and 
  the benefits of the other are made.  Interestingly, the reverse features
  comparision has been entirely omitted -- clusters have features that
  hot-plugging does not, and hot-plugging has some liabilities.

  An OpenVMS Cluster is a construct that I would expect that most Sun 
  folks would not be particularly familiar with -- for example, a cluster 
  is a good way to provide redundency for CPU failures, as well as for 
  other problems (erroneously disconnecting the main power is one classic 
  example) that can arise.  Clusters provide the ability to have wholely 
  independent systems with full and transparent file-level access 
  coordination including read- and write-sharing -- mechanisms for the 
  reliable election of a "primary" application are readily available, as 
  are mechanisms for detecting the failover of a node or an application 
  -- without requiring any failure processing on the node or in the 
  application.

:If I want to remove a CPU from a E10K I hot plug the 
:CPU, this can be as little as one CPU and will never 
:be more than 4. If you want to remove a CPU in a VAXcluster 
:you need to shut the node down, if you have a two node VAXcluster 
:then you have just removed half your resource to repair the 
:CPU.

  This is quite true -- hot-plugging is definitely a potentially faster 
  solution to an isolated failure -- one minute restart to, say, three
  or four minutes to restart.

  As mentioned earlier, clustering and hot-plugging are seperate approaches,
  with differing trade-offs.  Seperate nodes in an OpenVMS Cluster are far 
  more capable of riding over hardware faults such as a CPU or memory failure 
  immediately "underneath" a critical application or even "underneath" the 
  operating system itself -- hot-plugging does not address these failure 
  modes.  In other words, a hardware failure in one member does not effect 
  the other members of the cluster, and does not effect the ability of the 
  other cluster members to continue full read/write file-level sharing, and 
  to provide reliable mechanisms for both mastership and failover. 

  Repairs in a cluster can progress without disturbing the other existing 
  member nodes -- even operations such as operating system upgrades can 
  occur without shutting down the cluster or the applications running on it. 

  Having large systems is sometimes characterized as the "one-egg, one 
  basket" approach -- clustering avoids this on OpenVMS, as do the more 
  loosely coupled approaches available on both platforms such as NFS.
  There are certainly times when having a large system is very beneficial 
  -- the ideal system has both high performance uniprocessor speed, has 
  minimal SMP overhead, and has massive bandwidth among the processors. 
  Factors such as reliability and time-to-repair are also of interest -- 
  though factors such as the availability of parts can also come into play
  when determining the total time to repair.

  I've seen memory fail incrementally and start logging recoverable errors,
  I've seen random single-bit errors, but I can't say I've seen processors
  fail incrementally -- in my experience, they usually fail "hard".  (And
  hard CPU failures have repercussions with the operating system processing,
  operating system and application interlock processing, etc. -- having a
  now-deceased CPU own a "bitlock" construct or otherwise (formerly) occupied 
  executing an operating system critical path can be a major bummer.)

:It will take 90 seconds to remove a board from an E10K
:a couple of minutes to replace the CPU and 60 seconds 
:to bring the CPU and board back on line.

  Compared to the performance of the CPU stop and restart within an 
  OpenVMS Galaxy, these times are rather slower -- though the hardware
  under the OpenVMS Galaxy does not currently support hot-swapping, and
  the underlying "E10K" hardware may well be the direct cause of the 
  rather unexpectedly long shutdown and restart times.  (Pushing a
  processor from one instance to another within an OpenVMS Galaxy 
  typically requires well under a second...)

:How long 
:does it take you to shut the node down, repair 
:it and re-boot it. A bit longer than that I think.

  In the case of shutting down a cluster node and rebooting it -- for
  the sake of argument I will assume the repair times of the two systems
  are similar -- is rather longer than shutting down a processor in an 
  OpenVMS Galaxy or (apparently, not having tried it) in a Sun "E10K" 
  platform.  The end result of both approaches is the same, though the 
  mechanism using the reboot is slower -- is the difference between a
  (say) two or three minute reboot or a one minute reboot critical?
  In some cases it is, in some it is not -- you make your trade-off
  between the cost and the performance and the features.

  Fault tolerance comes in a range of levels, in order of increasing
  tolerance:

    o seperate nodes:

      Pushes the design and failover into the application software.
      I include NFS-style "clusters" -- those systems and configurations 
      without distributed lock management support.

        possible on all platforms.

    o clustering:

      Minimal or no design overhead required of the software.

        Available on both OpenVMS VAX and Alpha.
        Not available on Sun Solaris.

    o Seperate "instances" of the operating system:

        Supported on OpenVMS Alpha: OpenVMS Galaxy provides software 
	controlled configuration of processors -- the system can be
        reconfigured without being rebooting.  Minimal or no design 
	overhead required of the software.

        I do not know the current support for this on Sun platforms.
        (I have seen references to hardware partitioning, where a 
	reboot is required.)

    o hot-swapping:

      requires some level of software support and underlying (and 
      typically complex) hardware support...

        possible on certain VAX systems, not currently supported on Alpha.
	Possible on Compaq Tandem?
        Supported on Sun E10K.

      NB: Storage hot-swapping is typically available from most vendors.

    o hardware fault tolerance:

      the best features with the price tag to match

        Possible on certain VAX systems, not currently supported on Alpha.
	Available on Compaq Tandem platforms.
        Apparently not supported on E10K.

  As should be stated, the more tolerant the platform to errors -- either
  through manual detection and resolution or through automatic detection
  and failover -- typically the more expensive.

