Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> 
> Chris wrote:
> >
> > Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> >
> > > The VMS Cluster
> > > approach has a very coarse granularity. Its
> > > the node or nothing.
> > >
> >
> > So, with Sun you hot swap a new cpu module, with vax you bring another
> > node into the cluster - the net effect is the same - so what are you
> > driving at ?.
> >
> 
> The net effect is not the same.
> 
> If I want to remove a CPU from a E10K I hot plug the
> CPU, this can be as little as one CPU and will never
> be more than 4. If you want to remove a CPU in a VAXcluster
> you need to shut the node down, if you have a two node VAXcluster
> then you have just removed half your resource to repair the
> CPU.
> 
> It will take 90 seconds to remove a board from an E10K
> a couple of minutes to replace the CPU and 60 seconds
> to bring the CPU and board back on line. How long
> does it take you to shut the node down, repair
> it and re-boot it. A bit longer than that I think.
> 

Well, it's not quite fair to be talking VAX here, but rest assured that
Hot-Swap of Alpha CPUs will eventually be available - for as little
value that it adds, aside from marketing.

First of all, CPUs don't fail often, unless they are DOA to start with. 
The only really useful error that can allow a CPU to come down without
shutting down the entire system - is a high correctable cache error
rate.  Here you actually can get a idea that something is failing, and
your CPU may not be operating at peak efficiency.  A catestrophic CPU
failure has a number of problems - one is that you still need to be able
to flush its caches.  In general, the CPU still has to minimally be able
to creep along enough to extract itself from the SMP state of the
system, flush its caches, and tell the system that it is quieced and can
be powered off.

Hot-inswap, or hot-upgrade is much more useful.  That is, adding an
additional CPU, or upgrading to perhaps a faster CPU.  The first is
relatively trivial provided hardware support for inswapping a board, the
second is typically limited by the system design.

In addition, for your Daytona 500 pitstop speed 2-1/2 minute downtime
(damn fast I might add, congrats) *nothing* gets done.  Dropping a node
out of a cluster does not interrupt the continued operation of the other
members in the cluster.  A distributed application would have failed
over and the *users* would have percieved *no* downtime.


> > >
> > > Wouldn't you prefer to be able to swap out
> > > a failing SIMM module before it fails. most
> > > customers I deal with would. Granted you
> > > can do this using VMS but you need to
> > > shut the whole node down.
> > >
> >
> > > a failing simm module before it fails...
> >
> 
> > So we have precognition now as well ?...
> 
> No you predict from the bit error rate that
> the SIMM is going to fail.
> 


Hot-swap of memory is a pretty tough thing.  Congrats.  It's probably
less of a worry that the single-bit ECC corrected error rate is
indicative of an impending SIMM failure, but it does indicate that
performance is degraded.  So, how do you migrate the data out of an
arbitrary page (really, I'm curious?  Do you have no applications or
system code that is wired to physical addresses)?  Or is thiarbitrary page (really, I'm curious?  Do you have no applications or
system code that is wired to physical addresses)?  Or is this only
possible for, say, application memory?  How do you handle a multi-bit
uncorrectable memory error, on say, a page of kernel data structures?

Again, because you are not a VMS user, and think in terms of a "system"
and not in terms of the "cluster is the system", this appears to be a
bigger problem to you that perhaps it is.  I can *solve* the problem in
hardware, at a cost, and guarantee that *no* memory will *ever* fail
with an uncorrectable error, and allow memory to be hot-swapped -
without effecting SW.  But there *is* a HW cost.  You may actually see
things like this as memory sizes approach a terabyte.

What happens on an uncorrectable error on address data on the E10K's
crossbar (or whatever the system bus happens to be?) - do you continue
to run?  What happens if a bug (gasp) occurs in a device driver (say in
an obscure error path) that causes corruption of some arbitrary set of
pages?  What happens when the system detects some inconsistant state
that indicates a bug, corruption, or unknown hardware malfunction. 
Since the system-is-the-system, how do you contain the fault?  Do you
bring the entire 64-CPU system down because a CPU stops responding (due
to some catestrophic failure)?  How do you guarantee the integrity of
the system under a serious fault (hardware or software)?



