This might be referring to "On Some Associative, Parallel and Analog
Computations," (with S. Papert), @i[Associative Information Techniques,] E.L.
Jacks, ed., American Elsevier Publishing, Inc., 1971, pp.  27-47.

Umm, as I recall it, this simple idea was based on just looking at
conditional branches.  Suppose a computer goes a mean of K steps before
hitting a conditional branch (that is, one that depends on what has been
computed in that interval).  Then, in any straightforward parallel speedup
scheme, if you're trying to 'look-ahead' past that branch, you need two
processors to start anticipating those two branches.  (You also need to
'guess' how to initialize those processes--but let's assume optimistically
that you don't need to change any parameters.  Not likely, of course.)

Then, if you try to go even faster, you must double the number of processors
every K units of lookahead time, hence the gloomy order of logN speedup for N
processors.

Of course, this assumes that there's no smarter way to compute the thing,
e.g., by using huge tables, etc.  But then we gave an informal argument,
based on Shannon's proof that most circuits cannot be simplified (that is,
rewritten as composite functions of simpler functions)--hence, in general,
there do not exist 'smarter' ways to rewrite arbitrary programs.  However,
such a conclusion would not generally apply to "interesting" processes with
meaningful structures, so it would be hard to formally prove any such thing
about actual programs.

