Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> 
> No you predict from the bit error rate that
> the SIMM is going to fail.
>

Well, I wouldn't apply that terminology to memory system errors. But if
there are significant memory error rates on Sun servers in general to
_need_ such remedies as hot swap memory, there must be something wrong
with the system hardware design.

Memory errors on well designed modern systems are generally so low to be
statistically insignificant. Modern hardware is very reliable, more so
if it's burned in. It's probably several orders of magnitude more
reliable than the code that runs on it. What soft errors there are, few
and far between, should be more than adequately handled by the ecc. If
hardware does fail, it's usually because of stuff that has nothing to do
with inherent chip reliability - things like overheating due to fan
failure, for example.  
  
So, one wonders if all this hot swap hardware is really hot swap hype -
a paranoia inducing/relieving ruse to sell the client extra hardware
that he will never need. 

Hot swap hard drives maybe, better specced / hot swap psu's and fans,
but hot swap memory and cpu, not convinced. A lot of extra, expensive
hardware - for what gain ?. Obviously a marketing creation, not an
engineers...


Rgrds, Chris



