Actually, I find this constructive, but I have some follow-up:

> 
> You are also right to identify that there are a lot of
> failure conditions that would cause a CPU to crash
> completely. However once that CPU has crashed completely
> and been configured out of the system, you still need to
> replace it.
> 
> So to do this on a running system you need either shut the
> system down and replace the module and re-boot it or you need
> to hot swap the CPU. This is possible on an E10K as is replacing
> faulty I/O controllers and memory.
> 

The question is what type of failures allow a truly broken CPU to
actually be removed without requiring a restart because you cannot have
confidence in the continued execution of the system as a whole?

Say CPU_3 had taken out a lock on a critical section, and had started
modifying data, at which point the CPU itself fails - say a multi-bit
error in it's cache.  How can you remove this CPU and continue execution
- and how can you with confidence know that you can continue?  If the
CPU is not at least capable of an orderly shutdown - I can't myself
think of a way to do it.  At least in a monolithic SMP system (even with
distributed parts of the kernel).

Replacing a faulty controller is another one of those cases - the
controller has to have failed in a non-catestrophic way, such that you
can determine that nothing in the system has been compromised by the
failure.  In my experience, most controllers do not fail this way.  The
things that are most likely to be detectable as correctable errors tend
to involve the external connections, in which case you might see high
retry counts.

Memory that is suffering ECC correctable errors (and I disagree with
someone earlier, the more memory you have, the higher the risk of a
uncorrectable multi-bit error occuring) can be replaced - but that
requires that you either know what the memory in the SIMM is being used
for - or you are quiecing the system, snap-shotting the memory contents,
replacing the memory, reloading the contents and continuing.  What
happens if you get a uncorrectable error on a page that is not
replicated (or even if replicated, do you have the logic and confidence
to be able to restore the page from a replicated copy)?

> 
> You can do hot additions of more CPUs, memory and I/O
> controllers to a running Sun, you cannot currently change
> the CPU clock speeds on a running system.
> 

Yup.  That is a problem.  A rolling upgrade to faster CPUs would be
nirvana for customers, but it is very difficult, as it requires CPUs to
run in an asynchronous manner, or at fixed multiples of the system
clock.  Or it introduces some real interesting anomolies in SMP
operation.  Hot-inswap is cool.


> > In addition, for your Daytona 500 pitstop speed 2-1/2 minute downtime
> > (damn fast I might add, congrats) *nothing* gets done.  Dropping a node
> > out of a cluster does not interrupt the continued operation of the other
> > members in the cluster.  A distributed application would have failed
> > over and the *users* would have percieved *no* downtime.
> >
> 
> This is fine, Sun also supports distributed DBMS applications for
> example Oracle Parallel Server, Informix XPS e> >
> 
> This is fine, Sun also supports distributed DBMS applications for
> example Oracle Parallel Server, Informix XPS etc. This however
> is not an option for a very large chunk of Suns and Digitals
> customer base because the applications layered on top of the
> DBMS do not support any form of clustering. BTW I am
> not in any way comparing DBMS parallel clustering with
> OVMS clusters.
> 
> For exampel int the ERP/GRP space none of the major applications
> suites SAP R3, BaaN, PeopleSoft or Oracle supports any form of
> distributed
> cluster on any platfom except SAP who support IBM Sysplex for
> SAP Parallel. For these applications hot swap within a node rather
> that node swap within a cluster is currently the only supported
> option.
> 

Down time is down time.  If you can afford full interruption of service
(and lets be more realistic than 2-1/2 minutes to quiece, pull, replace,
and resume operation) - then this is OK, otherwise you need some type of
distribution if you do not want any interruption of service.


> > > > >
> > > > > Wouldn't you prefer to be able to swap out
> > > > > a failing SIMM module before it fails. most
> > > > > customers I deal with would. Granted you
> > > > > can do this using VMS but you need to
> > > > > shut the whole node down.
> > > > >
> > > >
> > > > > a failing simm module before it fails...
> > > >
> > >
> > > > So we have precognition now as well ?...
> > >
> > > No you predict from the bit error rate that
> > > the SIMM is going to fail.
> > >
> >

>...


I'm still interested in how a multi-bit uncorrectable memory error is
handled.  I'm curious how you handle it.


> > What happens on an uncorrectable error on address data on the E10K's
> > crossbar (or whatever the system bus happens to be?) - do you continue
> > to run?  What happens if a bug (gasp) occurs in a device driver (say in
> > an obscure error path) that causes corruption of some arbitrary set of
> > pages?  What happens when the system detects some inconsistant state
> > that indicates a bug, corruption, or unknown hardware malfunction.
> > Since the system-is-the-system, how do you contain the fault?  Do you
> > bring the entire 64-CPU system down because a CPU stops responding (due
> > to some catestrophic failure)?  How do you guarantee the integrity of
> > the system under a serious fault (hardware or software)?
> 
> The E10K has 2 data crossbars and 4 address switches If
> an Address bus goes bad, its blacklisted, the system continues
> to run but without the failed address bus. There are 3 more
> to go.
> 

But how do you continue execution of the system?  How do you know the
system hasn't been compromised?  I can see shutting down, reconfiguring,
and restarting the system.  Or is it a cross-your-fingers-and-pray that
"most" of the time nothing of value has been corrupted?  That would
scare a lot of people.  Perhaps the crossbars and address switches are
redundant, and you can fail one of these and run in a degraded mode (can
you hot-swap one of these?), but how do you handle a software or
hardware bug that poses potential corruption?  How do you power down a
unresponsive CPU and guarantee the integrity of the system?


























