>         suck does it?  After all, that same OS pumps out wonderful numbers
>         in the UE6000.  Surely the UE10000 should pump out higher tpmCs with
>         64 CPUs, correct?  No.  Latency is introduced due to memory coherency
>         issues.  Partition that UE10000 into 16 instances and run OPS, your
>         tpmCs skyrocket.

TPC-C benchmarks have not until recently been a E10K priority. This 
does of course allow the standard E10K long latency knock off to 
rear its head. There are a couple of easy answers to this. Firstly 
the E10K latency is not that long, its longer than a low/medium loaded 
E6500 but under load it does not degrade significantly, it is also 
better than many other competative systems the HP V-Series and 
the 8400 come to mind. 

It also does not seem to damage the E10Ks ability to run other 
more complex OLTP applications/benchmarks at very high transaction 
rates. After all the E10K does have by far the best SAP SD numbers 
also returning the lowest average response time. Not bad for a 
system whose latency supposedly cripples it for OLTP. 

It would of course be an option to run OPS with multiple DBMS 
instances on the same machine, Sequent did this apparently 
for their current TPC-C numbers, but then you are expecting 
all your customers to use OPS which they may for a variety 
of reasons be unable to do.

One other reason that Sun has not run big TPC-C TPM numbers on the 
E10K historically is that Solaris 2.5.1 and 2.6 did not support 
SGA's bigger than 3.75 GB. One way around this would have been
as you suggest to use OPS as Sequent have done within a system. 
Alternatively one could use Solaris 7 and a single OS instance. 
 

