Bob Koehler wrote:
> 
> In article <366EA8B4.3F249657@uk.sun.nospam.com>, Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com> writes:
> >
> >OpenVMS customers may be forced to move to something
> >else. One good example being SAP R2 which does run
> >on OpenVMS and R3 which does not.
> >
> 
> Nonsense.  Don't need SAP, not R2 and not R3.  Why did you ignore my
> statement that what I need is a good compiler and a choice of OS?

I am sure that a compiler may be all you need, but users with 
your kind of requirements will not sustain the OpenVMS 
market. Many commercial companies now avoid any inhouse 
software development where ever possible and if they 
are doing development much of it is more assembly 
than basic construction. 

I work mainly in the wholesale banking market, one 
commercial environment where leading companies have 
in the past done a great deal of development. One I
know of had its own programming language, compilers etc
many have in-house data/message busses. But over the last 
5 years most of these companies are now moving to a 
package approach.

You may not need the SAPs, BaaNs and Oracles of this 
world but they account for a huge chunk of what is 
run on any system and not having them on your 
platform limits its market and thus its revenues.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



