Hoff Hoffman wrote:
> 
>   Continuing with the line of thought that Andrew Harrison started me
>   thinking on over in the comp.os.vms newsgroup...
> 
>   Remember that the real reason that customers often start these sorts
>   of discussions are to look for a new system to resolve some existing
>   problem.  Vendors then being to discuss the benefits of their system(s)
>   over those of the competitors...  One of the tools of this system
>   comparison is something I call benchmarketing...
> 

I can only assume that you do not speak for either CPQ or Digital 
as you previous employer since they were both involved in the  
biggest Benchmarketing excercises of the decade.

Had you forgotten Microsoft scalability day. CPQ, MS and 
Digital trotted out a set of marketing marks that have had 
almost no parallel in the history of benchmarking. 

There is no point in rehashing the past but Digital a once 
honest company did not cover themselves with glory by even 
being on the same stage as MS let alone being a active participant. 

I particularly liked the MS Exchange Server benchmark which 
running on an Alpha supposedly showed that Exchange could 
support 50,000 active mail users. The fine print was that 
they were all actually reading from to my memory 230 usernet 
news archives, hardly 50,000 users each having read,write access 
to their own mailboxes.

There are also a number of other particulary sorry examples 
the Terra Server and the Image database used for the scalability 
day demos being horrid examples.

I didn't expect much from CPQ or MS but I did from Digital.


>   To properly compete in the "benchmark game", first look for any really
>   big benchmark number you have, where the competitors have a smaller
>   number -- it doesn't matter that the customer will never do anything
>   even remotely similar, it's the best-looking number that matters.  One
>   example is to look at one component of the system that is faster than
>   the equivilent component in the competing system -- differences in system
>   memory architectures and memory caching schemes, and the resulting
>   differences in memory access is one example I've occasionally seen used
>   for competitive purposes.  The aggregate bandwidth of a cross-bar vs that
>   of a bus design is another.
>
>   Also make sure you downplay any area that the competitor might have
>   an advantage in -- one common technique I've seen used here is "nobody
>   needs 64 bits of addressing".  That's certainly quite true.  What is often
>   neglected when this statement is posited is that there are a number of
>   applications out there that need the 33rd or 34th bit, and these just
>   don't exist on a 32 bit system.  Clustering is another area that I've
>   noticed this -- one vendor offers a two-node shared-nothing "cluster".
>   OpenVMS has had full write-sharing for a decade, and customers with
>   clusters well beyond the supported limit  of 96 nodes are around.  File
>   system reliability is also an area I've mentioned before -- it's easy to
>   go fast (remember the "<insert car> with the solid rocket booster" urban
>   legend?), but it's not nearly so easy to go fast reliably.
> 

Oh good so you never read the Digital VLDB 64 benchmark white papers.
You seem to have forgotten that the whole DU 64 bit marketing campaign 
was based on a set of marketing marks that had such big holes in that 
you could drive a bus through them.

Sure Sun downplayed 64 bit as did HP and IBM, but when the basis 
of all the Digital claims stemmed from a set of marketing marks 
that wasn't difficult.

Incedentaly I would be really intested in anything other than Marketing 
marks that demonstrated the big win that VLDB 64 imparted on DBMS's. 

Or is it your thesis that all benchmarks are really marketing 
marks.

>   People purchase computers to solve problems -- benchmarks are certainly
>   a factor, but few folks actually run benchmarks as their application(s).
>   Quite obviously, customers want the best value for the money -- but major
>   factors that play into the decision involve familiarity with the platform,
>   the availability of applications, and the time and effort involved to get
>   ported and running on the platform -- any major changes in these areas
>   can be expensive.  Also a factor is the time to write any necessary
>   application code -- most folks probably don't want to be writing and
>   supporting a file record management system if they can avoid it, for
>   instance.  Customers also want to see that the platforms can support
>   the environment(s) they expect to be moving into -- this can include
>   interoperation with other computer systems, requirements for minimal
>   footprint, simplicity of operation, compliance with industry standards
>   (defacto or otherwise), etc.
> 

I agree with you that customers buy systems to solve problems 
and in that light could you defend or explain Digitals involvement 
in MS scalability day.

Some benchmarks such as SAP SD serve dual purposes, purpose one is for 
the vendors who run the benchmark to crow about how fast they are 
running it. Purpose 2 is to enable SAP competency 
centers to calcualate what sized system a customer needs given 
a predicted load.

Many other customers also use benchmarks as a test of capability, 
they are not so concerned with how fast or otherwise you are 
but more that you have actually been able to run the benchmark 
at all. 

We see vendor shortlists based on say the fact that a vendor 
has run say TPC-D 300GB or 1TB. Or that say a platform 
has an Oracle TPC-C number. 

>   In my experience, wading through the marketing material and the FUD
>   (Fear, Uncertainty, and Doubt) -- looking for the nuggets of actual
>   content -- is one of the worst parts of the whole process of acquiring
>   a new computer system.  Remember, the real reason you came in the door
>   was to look for a new system.  The fact that you're punch drunk with
>   benchmarketing (or if you've been in the hospitality suite a little too
>   long, literally drunk :-) can often simply detract from resolving the
>   very reason(s) you started the whole purchasing process...
> 
>   Remember why you came in the door.  (And keep your hand on your wallet. :-)
> 
>         --
> 
>   PS: per one of Andrew Harrison's threads that started this whole thought
>   off, the GS140 supports up to fourteen (14) 21264 processors.  Not forty
>   (40), as was incorrectly cited in that thread.  Up to 28 GB of memory.
>   The TurboLaser backplane (TLSB) is good for 1.2 gigabytes per second --
>   I've seen well over a gigabyte per second in actual communications use
>   between applications running in different OpenVMS Galaxy instances.
>   (Which might even beat the bandwidth of a truckload of floppies. :-)
> 

Incorrect, probably the wrong choice of wording. The GS140 
does as you quite rightly say support 14 CPUs not 40, however 
Digital/CPQ (you work for them right) used a 4 node DU cluster 
with 10 CPUs per node.

Given that a standard floppy holds 1.44 MB and a truck load 
is probably in the 10,000 mark I rather doubt that you have 
ever seen anything that beats the bandwidth of a truck load 
of floppies. latency is another matter.

