>failure.  In my experience, most controllers do not fail this way. 
<SNIP>

Fred is right, which is why every system that I've ever heard of which is 
ultrareliable depends on multiple SYSTEMS (not CPUs in one system).  VMS
clusters for instance, or the multiple redundant computers used on the
space shuttle. If memory serves, all 3 (?) do the same calculations all the
time, and if one ever disagrees with the other 2, then it is assumed to
have gone bad.  At least, in rough terms, that's how it works.

Now I can imagine you could sort of do the same thing within a multicpu
machine - replicate each problem 3 times, assign each thread a processor,
maybe a disk and disk controller, and a particular piece of memory, then
compare the results. Then you could possibly detect and correct certain
types of errors, but you'd need some other piece of hardware to keep
each intracomputer "node" from touching the contents of another, and if
that hardware fails, you're back at a nonrecoverable situation.  It seems a
lot less robust than using separate machines. 

>Yup.  That is a problem.  A rolling upgrade to faster CPUs would be
>nirvana for customers, but it is very difficult, as it requires CPUs to
>run in an asynchronous manner, or at fixed multiples of the system
>clock. 

We're already talking about hideously expensive machines here.  Sites that 
can afford these can presumably afford to rent a second one for a week, 
boot it into the cluster, migrate everything onto it, upgrade the original
machine, and migrate everything back - no down time.  In fact, many such
sites probably already run multiple (multiple-cpu) machines in their
clusters, so they don't need any more hardware to do the upgrade, but they
will have to pass (quickly) through a period with reduced capacity. 

