>I inherited 2 DEC 400mhz machines with 128 Mb RAM...
>they are the file servers for approx. 10 disks totaling
>80Gb...

Ethernet? FDDI? UFS? AdvFS? Release?

>anyway, the nfs performance sucks!

How well does rlogin/telnet/ftp run?  ttcp?  Please provide a numeric
vaule and units for "sucks".

>  running nfsstat
>shows that there are a lot of nfs timeouts...

That generally suggests you should look deeper.  "netstat -s", "netstat -is"
are useful.  Look for CRC and checksum errors.


>I'm new to DEC UNIX and NFS, so what sorts of things
>can I look at as potential problems?

Many sites are surprised to discover that NFS accounts for the majority
of their traffic is NFS.  Also, NFS is fairly talkative about its
problems.  Together, many "NFS problems" are often elsewhere.  NFS is
a wonderful system exerciser, but lousy diagnostic tool, except for alerting
you that there is a problem.

On NFS thing worth doing is to check small messages.  "du", especially
on NFS V2 is good at generating lookups and getattrs.  Sometimes those
work well, but big messages (readdir, read, write) can clog routers or
other equipment that can't handle messages with the minimum spacing
between them.
