On 11 Dec 1998, David Mathog of "Biology Division, Caltech" responded
to what Fred Kleinsorge said:
> >
>_> The question is what type of failures allow a truly broken CPU to
>_> actually be removed without requiring a restart because you cannot have
>_> confidence in the continued execution of the system as a whole? ...
>_> Replacing a faulty controller is another one of those cases....
>
>
> Fred is right, which is why every system that I've ever heard of which is 
> ultrareliable depends on multiple SYSTEMS (not CPUs in one system). 
> ...
> Now I can imagine you could sort of do the same thing within a multicpu
> machine - replicate each problem 3 times, assign each thread a processor,
> maybe a disk and disk controller, and a particular piece of memory, then
> compare the results. 


Dr. Mathog's imagination is not so different from the daily experience
of a large number of people in the world who regard their computing as
a critical activity.  They work for organizations that do have
computers built from multiple processors that perform the same
computations, have their individual outputs compared, and emit a
"system" output based on the comparison.  One such group of people are
the customers of my employer:  Stratus Computer.

There is a large body of technical literature on fault-tolerant
computer architectures, and there are several ways to build a
redundant-hardware machine.  What he describes is a three-way voting
scheme, which is certainly a workable plan.  Other schemes are
also possible, such as the "pair-and-partner" architecture used by
Stratus since 1982.

In pair-and-partner, each "logical CPU" that is visible to the user
actually consists of 4 physical CPU chips, arranged as two pairs (call
them "pair A" and "pair B").  All four CPUs execute an identical code
path, and their outputs are given as input to comparator circuits.  If
one CPU chip in pair A has a problem and gives an output that
disagrees with the other pair-A chip, the pair is deemed "broken" and
the outputs of the two CPUs are taken off the bus.  However, keep in
mind that the two pair-B CPU chips are still operating, and they
don't miss a beat--they continue the computation.  Nothing has to
"fail over".  No special software is invoked.  No transactions are
rolled back.  The remaining CPU chips just keep going.  (Well, after
a while they notice that pair A is broken, so they phone home and
order replacement parts.  No kidding.)  

One property of the processor chips necessary for pair-and-partner is
that their operation must be deterministic, so that all 4 chips in a
"logical processor" can work in lockstep.  The current generation of
Stratus machines uses PA-RISC processor chips from Hewlett-Packard,
which permit this.

(Of course, you need other parts of the computer to be duplexed, as
well:  memories, data busses, disks, power, I/O processors, etc.)


> Sites that 
> can afford these can presumably afford to rent a second one for a week, 
> boot it into the cluster, migrate everything onto it, upgrade the original
> machine, and migrate everything back--no down time.


It is true that the hardware costs somewhat more than the equivalent
single-chip implementation, but in many cases total cost of ownership
can be less than a backup-machine fail-over-based scheme.  (Consider
having to buy just one Oracle license instead of two....)

(I think, however, that Dr. Mathog's definition of "no down time" is
rather generous.  If computations are being migrated around, surely
there is some discontinuity during the changeover?)

My point is not to make a sales pitch but to show that such highly
reliable computer architectures do exist and can be commercially
successful.  Some years ago, even Digital tried to do a similar
product (the so-called VAX/FT machines), but, like so many of DEC's
efforts, they didn't quite get the recipe right, and the VAX/FT never
achieved the hoped-for market penetration.

