In article <74mmmn$3vt@news1.panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
> That's almost *exactly* how a SCSI disk and controller work.
> 
> DSSI is just SCSI with some extra commands, made deliberately electrically
> incompatible for the usual reasons.

Care to back this up with some concrete evidence?  My understanding
was that DSSI used Digital's MSCP protocol, which claims to have some
advantages in bus utilization over SCSI, or at least over the SCSI
implementations that were prevalent at the time DSSI was introduced.
Since MSCP was already well established in the DEC world by the time
DSSI was introduced, it would have been natural to continue to use it
and would have resulted in considerable advantages in terms of reuse
of existing device support code and compatibility with the existing
I/O system.

DSSI has always been touted as using command queueing at the device
level - essentially tagged command queueing, but different in that
it has been supported since day one on DSSI devices, so device drivers
don't need to query the device to find if it's there.

Oh, and DSSI has supported cluster communications traffic from (almost)
the beginning, something that is still not being done with SCSI.

So I will grant you this: Digital introduced DSSI as their answer to
SCSI.  It had many similarities; but it also had value-added features
that made it a premium product compared to the SCSI "economy" versions
that were the competition.

