From: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.nospam (Hoff Hoffman)
Subject: Sun Solaris vs OpenVMS, Alpha vs SPARC, etc (was ...)
Date: 1998/12/07
Message-ID: <74gvcd$jhv@usenet.pa.dec.com>
X-Deja-AN: 419673028
Distribution: world
Organization: Compaq Computer Corporation
Reply-To: hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.sun.admin



  Andrew Harrison's (SUNUK) recent flurry of postings over in comp.os.vms 
  started me thinking about the comparisions among processor architectures,
  and even across operating system configuration choices...  Both the Sun
  Solaris and Compaq OpenVMS operating systems are obviously targeted at 
  different markets, but both are obviously quite capable -- the target
  markets are also obviously somewhat different, though there are overlaps.

  I'm not particularly familiar with the operating systems that run on 
  SPARC (other than Solaris, of course), though several vendors offer 
  operating systems for Alpha systems including Compaq (OpenVMS and UNIX),
  and Microsoft Windows NT.  As I am (obviously) most familiar with OpenVMS,
  I will primarily discuss that operating system here.

  The following material is probably "review" for many of the long-term
  readers here, but I thought the discussion of the trade-offs might be 
  interesting...
  
  Sun has chosen to maintain and extend its own SPARC architecture, while
  Compaq has chosen to build OpenVMS for and operate on Alpha systems (and 
  on the VAX predecessors).  Both architectures are obviously quite capable.
  One salient difference between SPARC and Alpha is in terms of processor 
  performance -- information posted in various newsgroups including 
  comp.os.vms and elsewhere indicates that Alpha-based processor modules 
  (either the older 21164A EV56 or the newer 21264 EV6) have higher 
  performance than a SPARC equivalent.  Various SPARC systems are presently 
  available with more processor modules configured, however.

  As has been discussed recently in the comp.os.vms newsgroup, symmetric 
  multiprocessing (SMP) can improve aggregate performance, but something 
  often called Amdahl's Law eventually arises.  Though I am paraphrasing 
  here, Amdahl has stated that the performance scaling one could expect 
  from adding processors to an SMP configuration directly corresponds to 
  the amount of work that could operate in parallel.  In other words, most 
  any computer environment would eventually encounter a critical path that 
  effectively limits the number of processors, either in the application 
  or in the operating system itself.  Adding processors eventually won't
  help once the "knee" is reached.

  In practice, the performance "knee" can be anywhere from one processor 
  (single-stream) to infinity (best case, minimal coordination) infinity.
  The "knee" is obviously heavily application dependent and also dependent 
  on the operating system.  Obviously, having any processors available 
  above the "knee" obviously does not contribute to the performance of the 
  application -- those processors will be idle.

  Once the "knee" is reached (in terms of SMP processor performance), the 
  level of parallelism must be increased in the system or in the appliation 
  (or both) -- if such an increase is even possible -- or the existing 
  processors in the configuration must be replaced with faster processors. 

  That said: there are certainly algorithms and applications around that 
  are highly parallel and that make good use of configurations with many 
  processors, but most applications and most operating systems do not lend
  themselves to extensive parallelism.  (The parallel processing benchmarks 
  are one obvious example of an application that is good at using processors.)

  Interestingly, SMP was something that OpenVMS pushed heavily into when 
  platforms competing with the VAX line showed better performance than was
  available on VAX -- when the VAX wasn't the fastest processor around.  
  Various competing vendors then decried SMP as an inferior solution to the 
  better scaler performance available on other platforms -- there are 
  arguments to be made both ways, of course.  (The arguments chosen usually 
  depend on whether the vendor has the "faster processors" or the "more 
  processors" configuration, obviously.)

  Put simply -- for single-stream performance -- nothing can beat the 
  faster processor.  (Guess which side of the argument I'm on. :-)

  Another area that OpenVMS pushed into back in the days when the the
  underlying hardware (VAX) had lower performance than competing platforms 
  was clustering -- clustering was pursued for many of the same reasons 
  that SMP was of interest.  The addition of nodes to an OpenVMS Cluster 
  is a simple affair, and the nodes can boot and fail seperately.  Nodes 
  can be configured with a common system disk, or with multiple system 
  disks.  Individual nodes in a cluster can have the local operating system 
  upgraded and even entire systems can be replaced with a faster system, 
  but the cluster itself stays fully operational and fully available.  
  Further, nodes in a cluster can be hundreds of miles apart, meaning that 
  configurations can survive problems ranging from powerfailures to the 
  wholesale destruction of the systems of one "lobe" of the cluster.  Cluster 
  configurations with 96 nodes -- SMP or otherwise -- are fully supported 
  on OpenVMS.  The same disks and files can be visible on all nodes, and 
  the same file access interlocking and coordination features are standard.

  OpenVMS has recently also chosen to make use of processors through the 
  ability to operate with multiple instances -- each instance of OpenVMS 
  operating in an OpenVMS Galaxy configuration is effectively a separate 
  system in which OpenVMS is running.  The OpenVMS Galaxy software allows
  processors in an instance to be "pushed" into any other instance entirely
  under software control, based on user-specified factors such as the depth 
  of the computable scheduling queue or even the time of day.  The same
  code that supports starting and stopping processors in an OpenVMS SMP 
  system was the basis for migrating processors within an OpenVMS Galaxy.

  OpenVMS Galaxy allows applications to side-step Amdahl's law, and to make 
  better use of the available processors in the configuration by bringing up 
  additional instances as required.  (An OpenVMS Galaxy could be viewed as
  a way to allow multiple instances of a critical path to operate entirely
  in parallel.)  As the loading of each instance changes, the processors 
  can be retargeted as required without rebooting and even without system 
  management intervention. Each instance can be clustered, or can be running 
  standalone.

  Also available within an OpenVMS Galaxy are backplane speed interconnects 
  between each instance, for use as shared memory and for communications 
  interconnects.  (I have seen real applications communicating among clients
  running in separate instances at transfer rates of well over a gigabyte 
  per second -- and these tests were not on a leading-edge hardware bus.) 

  When compared with OpenVMS Galaxy, an OpenVMS Cluster provides lower-speed 
  interconnects, a trade-off between the performance of the interconnect 
  with the relative hardware isolation.

  As should be obvious, applications that are heavily parallelized and are 
  largely or completely independent can operate just as nicely in a cluster 
  or even in a network of nodes as they can in an OpenVMS Galaxy or in a 
  single SMP system -- where one would choose an OpenVMS Cluster or an 
  OpenVMS Galaxy configuration over a network of nodes involves applications 
  that need to share data.
  
  Speaking of file systems and sharing read or read and write access, most 
  operating systems tend to provide rather limited support for record 
  structures and record management, requiring additional software and/or 
  user programming.  OpenVMS has provided the ability to share access to 
  files, and also supports various file organizations including indexed 
  files, and to interlock access.  Once seldom sees "turd" files used 
  for interlocking on OpenVMS, save for on applications ported over to
  OpenVMS from other platforms.  These "turd" files can be quite common 
  on other platforms.  (As should be obvious, I don't find "turd" files 
  a particularly elegant solution -- though I have worked on platforms 
  and in cases where they are the best solution of a bad lot.  Nor do
  I find the requirement that the application perform the interlocking
  for sharing elegant -- that's too much like work, and every application
  I've seen try it has implemented it slightly differently.)

  Other solutions are available to distribute applications across multiple
  systems are also often referenced -- these include the NFS, SMB, and other 
  protocols.  As should be obvious, OpenVMS and most every other vendor 
  supports these and various other protocols.  However, unlike an OpenVMS 
  cluster, these protocols to not provide coordination among applications 
  accessing the files -- hence the use of the "turd" file scheme -- and
  these do not provide for shared write access to files.

  The combination of an OpenVMS Galaxy, the raw processor performance of 
  Alpha systems, and the ability to cluster the configurations can be very 
  powerful and flexible -- while I am certainly not a disinterested party 
  -- I've seen nothing that even comes close.  (Andrew Harrison, I'm sure,
  will disagree.  :-)


 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
  note to those folks not contributing spam -- there is no ZZ in my address




