  smoke long ago.

  There are very few application systems that have no critical path 
  somewhere: as examples, consider applications that process a database 
  in parallel, or a series of applications that all write to seperate 
  disks.  Eventually, you will encounter a critical path -- it could be 
  in the I/O subsystem, in the database, or somewhere else. But eventually, 
  you encounter some task that you just can't perform in parallel fast 
  enough, and you hit the wall...

  Applications that led themselves to parallel execution will tend to have 
  fewer interdependencies -- crunching prime numbers is one example of a 
  task that has few interdependencies, and can be scattered across large 
  numbers of processors.  (Crunching prime numbers is a parallel application
  that also does not particularly require the communications bandwidth 
  available within a Sun server or a Compaq GS140, however.)

Fred Kleinsorge wrote:

:  Maybe he meant Minsky's Conjecture ;-)

  Is that any relation to the Pavlovian effect?

