hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.nospam (Hoff Hoffman) writes:

>  OpenVMS Galaxy allows applications to side-step Amdahl's law, and to make 
>  better use of the available processors in the configuration by bringing up 
>  additional instances as required.  (An OpenVMS Galaxy could be viewed as
>  a way to allow multiple instances of a critical path to operate entirely
>  in parallel.)  As the loading of each instance changes, the processors 
>  can be retargeted as required without rebooting and even without system 
>  management intervention. Each instance can be clustered, or can be running 
>  standalone.


This is, of course, only partially true.  It allows you to side step software
bottlenecks, it doesn't allow you to sidestep hardware bottlenecks.

It's hardware bottlenecks that are often most of the problem, as in
the backplane bandwidth is sometimes limited or teh off-node NUMA latency
simply becomes too big.

If you can make a task run faster by splitting the OS in two instances
on teh same hardware, this really only means one thing: the scalability of
the OS sucks.  In other words: there is a software bottleneck in the OS
that limits the number of processors that can be used effectively
for a certain task.  For some reason, such a task can be split up in multple
independent tasks in such a way that, even though more communication outside
the single system image is needed, that extra communication is cheaper
than living with the bottleneck.  It must be quite a bottleneck then.

>  Also available within an OpenVMS Galaxy are backplane speed interconnects 
>  between each instance, for use as shared memory and for communications 
>  interconnects.  (I have seen real applications communicating among clients
>  running in separate instances at transfer rates of well over a gigabyte 
>  per second -- and these tests were not on a leading-edge hardware bus.) 

SO two instances can shuffle pages between OS nodes?  How independent
does that make two such instances?  It appears to me that if one such
instance fails or malfunctions, it can take out other instances
and/or fatally wound them.

(I'm not sure if you're also talking about new hardware with cross bar
technology)

>  The combination of an OpenVMS Galaxy, the raw processor performance of 
>  Alpha systems, and the ability to cluster the configurations can be very 
>  powerful and flexible -- while I am certainly not a disinterested party 
>  -- I've seen nothing that even comes close.  (Andrew Harrison, I'm sure,
>  will disagree.  :-)

All hardware/OS combinations are moving targets.  Next year will see
intersting products, I'm sure.

Casper
