> Err, I thought RAS means "reliability, availability, serviceability". Such
> things as redundant hardware down to the wall plug, hot-swappable components
> everywhere, generally high MTBF and low MTTR. A complex mix of hardware and
> software, and even more distributed when you throw disaster tolerance into the
> mix. Again, doesn't seem possible as an add-on.

Of course "reliability" is enhanced by minimizing the number of parts
(see the old space launch analogy).  So designing in extra circuitry
to provide this is a dubious proposition.  This is exactly the same
argument as the "fewer lines of OS code is more reliable" discussion.
I am not saying no manufacturer should ever load up on hot-swappable
features, but rather that no customer should ever have a knee-jerk
reaction that hot-swappable is better without fully considering their
position.

Hot swappable features may be mainly of interest to Unix customers,
but reading this thread in comp.os.vms I would mention that to a
great extent VMS users don't need that because they can take down
a single node in the cluster and swap in a whole other node without
upsetting ongoing operations.  Cluster-aware databases, like Oracle Rdb,
take this without a problem.  If it really is replacement of a whole
box, rather than a CPU upgrade, a VMS customer would likely add the
new one before removing the old, floorspace consideration permitting.

I can see swapping _in_ all hardware, and I can see swapping _out_
disk drives that were part of a shadow set, but I doubt that Solaris,
VMS, or any other mainstream operating system will soon be able to
handle swapping _out_ of arbitrary memory, since it contains a lot
of data for running programs, including the OS.  So hot swappable
hardware would not seem to count for much when it comes to memory.
Swapping out memory you have never used is not very interesting,
as I can buy a cheaper plastic storage box for spare memory at a
discount store than you would pay for _any_ manufacturer's CPU
cabinet.

Larry Kilgallen



