> 	21264 in the mean time: [...]

Well Andy, "hasn't slipped any futher" is a euphemism for "has
remained stuck in its totally non-existant state" n'est-ce pas?
You know what?  The super-duper bazillion MHz chip that I dreamt of a
couple of years ago hasn't slipped any further either.  Great, huh?

Let's see.  We were promised 600 MHz UltraSPARC III's for this Summer.
Now that *would* have been impressive but it's a good thing nobody
believed it in the first place since it never materialised.  Just a
few days ago Sun managed to trumpet about sampling 400 Mhz UltraSPARC II's.
Bwa ha ha!

Whereas "in the mean time" 21264 systems have started shipping and
several vendors are now advertising dual 21264 systems at circa US$20K
(for December delivery although I wouldn't be surprised if that
slipped into January).


Just to compare some real-life speeds I came across in my work this
morning: pitting a 270 MHz UltraSPARC II versus a 500 Mhz Alpha 21164a
(two-year old technology I know, but Alpha's the only one to have a
new generation in those years...)  The Sparc was running assembly and
C compiled with acc.  The Alpha was running C compiled with gcc.  The
result was a factor of 5 in favour of the Alpha.  Nuff said.


Believe it or not I used to use Sun's a lot.  They work well and are
quite reliable.  Can't fault that.  But oh my God are they slow for my
work.  Alpha's also work well, are reliable plus they absolutely beat
the living daylights out of any Sparc.


I notice you couldn't find a machine to pit against the 633 MHz/$2795
Alpha workstation I was considering so I'll go ahead and order it.


Pity you couldn't even find some amusing repartee though, but that's
probably because you're a bit of a one-trick pony solely capable of
rehashing the some material over and over again.  It was pretty boring
the first time, but now it's getting very tedious indeed.

Have a day,
  Rob.



