weebuwaabu@hotmail.com wrote in message <74an22$pkt$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>...

>Hi, I just picked up a vax 4000-300 from surplus for $10.

That's a deal (for someone who wan't such a beast) ! Would love to take one
for that price.

>What kind of beast do I have here?

A VAX 4000-300 ;-)   32 bit, 8 VUPS (that's about 8 MIPS). QBUS, 2
integrated DSSI busses (some kind of early SCSI)

> How do I get inside this thing?

Screwdriver ! The "slots" on top ar for drives (RF series) or tapes (TK
series). Do you have any drives? The RF series is a DEC proprietary format.
Common drives you need:

RF31E-AF (300 MB), RF35E-AF (850 MB), RF36E-AF (1,6 GB), RF72E-AF (1 GB),
RF73E-AF (2GB), RF74E-AF (4GB).

As you noticed you need a "RFxE-AF". Those drives have mounting brackets for
a VAX4000-300 (BA400 cabinet).

Also, you need some kind of "external drive" to get the operating system
loaded (if you didn't have a drive already loaded). SCSI drives (CDROM,
TAPES) would only work with the appropriate adapter card (KZQSA).

>There is a what appears to be a terminal port, but it has
>a wierd, (dec propriatary, I assume) plug.

MMJ. Most terminals (even my Wyse) have such a connector. There are adapters
available to turn it into a RS232.

> Can I put linux on this, or some kind of unix?

No linux, NetBSD "maybe",  but I'm not sure about the current port. check
www.netbsd.org .
There is a free VMS license available from www.decus.org for members
(membership is free).
DIGITAL Unix (OSF/1, ULTRIX) also would work, but it's not free.


Also you should check out the FAQ at:
http://anacin.nsc.vcu.edu/~jim/mvax/mvax_faq.html

CU
Chris





