"Damien" <nospams369459@student.uq.edu.au> writes...
}What does FINDFAST actually do?? It seems to sit back waiting to suck up
}the processor and then does but for what??? And the amount of HDD accessing
}is enough to make you think of the song " THINGS THAT MAKE YOU GO
}HMMMMMM.........."

Aparently it is indexing all of your MS Office files. I don't think it is
just the file names and locations either. You know all that search stuff
available in the Open File dialog, particularly of you click the "Advanvced.."
button that you never use? Well, all of that info is stored with all of your
Office documents - author, title, comments, keywords, last saved date, last
printed date; all that stuff that is set from the Properties item on the File
menu that you also never use, as well as verious other properties of the file.
Some unknown number of things like this get indexed by FindFast.

Since you never use any of it, it isn't really doing anything useful for you
so you should turn it off - go to the Control Panel and run the FindFast thing
there and turn all the indexing off. By default it runs every 2 hours. On
a big full disk (and/or slow system or more than one disk) it can take
several minutes every two hours - or less time more frequently if you have
multiple disks and the indexing of them is at different times. If, by some
small miracle, you do occasionally take advantage of some of the things it
indexes, you can slow it down by specifying a longer interval than the default
of 2 hours.

Think you crossposted to enough news groups, most of which are probably
poor choices if you were expecting an answer?

--- Carl



