Definetely not the case


: *) PWS a does have some busses/devices not supported by dUNIX and/or OpenVMS.
: 	eg. IDE is still supported only for CD-ROMs (w/o Audio) on OpenVMS
: 	eg. the disk controller is different on PWS a and au for this reason.
: 	eg. the PS 4D50T graphic controller in my PWS does not work with VMS.
: 

This is partially true, but you can get a-series PWS' that were configured
with UNIX-supportable components.  I can't speak to VMS, but IDE is
supported for CD-ROM's under UNIX, the disk controller is a card which can
be changed (and is supported under NT, UNIX, and VMS), and the 4D50T is
definetely supported under UNIX.

The things to look out for on an a-series machine:

1) Video:  Only PowerStorm-series adapters are supported under UNIX, no
Matrox Millenium/Accel Graphics

2) SCSI:   You need a QLogic QLA-1040 based SCSI adapter.  We have had
success getting a non-Digital branded QLogic adapter to work, but they're
hard to find at times.  The Adaptec commonly shipped with a-series machines
is not supported by UNIX or VMS.


: The PWSau is built of not the cheapest devices on the street. It is built
: of devices supported by all three DEC opsys (Alpha NT, Digital UNIX, OpenVMS)
: and is therefore (unfortunately) more expensive than the PWS a.
: 
: So if the firmware is not the problem, your system disk (controller) might be
: it...
: 

Yup, that sounds reasonable.  In reality, you should be able to convert your
a-series machine over to an au-series for a minor amount of money, like
around $300 for a supported SCSI controller and $150-1250 for a PowerStorm
graphics, depending on how much you want to spend and how good a card you
want.

And I *have* seen a-series machines that did ship with the right equipment
to run UNIX (a KZP series SCSI & a 3D or 4D series PowerStorm card).  These
were typically 500a or 600a units, though.  Never seen a 433a sold with that
stuff originally, but it's possible.


